	[==[{"module":"gui","version":"1.0","system_module":"gui","criteria":[{"id":"gui-scale-not-offset-layout","order_index":1,"tier":"baseline","system_module":"gui","statement":"KILLER SYSTEM — RESPONSIVE SCALE LAYOUT. The interface's size and position are expressed as a function of the parent (UDim2 Scale, 0..1) so the whole layout re-proportions with the viewport, NOT in fixed pixel Offset that is correct only on the author's monitor. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to Scale as the sizing/positioning substrate: passing requires the build to state that Size and Position use Scale for the main layout, with Offset reserved ONLY for things that genuinely must not scale (UIStroke thickness, small crisp icons). A build that sizes/positions elements in pixel Offset (the engine default for inserted elements) FAILS — it is the single most-cited generic-UI tell ('looks fine on PC, squished/overflowing on mobile'). A build that commits to Scale-driven Size+Position passes at the architecture level; a paired matching AnchorPoint, scaled UICorner, and a device-emulator verification strengthen it toward full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"Raw inserted Roblox UI uses Offset and stays one fixed pixel size regardless of screen — that is exactly the floor and exactly the mean, the #1 'AI did it' signature. Scale is not a styling choice but the structural commitment that makes a layout hold its proportions across mobile/tablet/desktop/console. Grading the architectural commitment to Scale-as-substrate (rather than demanding a multi-device capture a design-stage build can't show) keeps this discriminating: a Scale-driven build clears it, a pixel-Offset build structurally cannot.","grading":{"pass":"Size and Position are driven by UDim2 Scale for the main layout, with Offset confined to genuinely non-scaling elements (UIStroke thickness, fixed small icons). Strengthened by matching AnchorPoint, scaled UICorner, and/or a device-emulator screenshot showing the layout holding proportions across phone/tablet/desktop.","partial":"Scale is used for some of the layout but key elements are still positioned/sized in pixel Offset, OR the commitment to Scale is stated but Offset leaks into the primary layout, leaving the proportions only partly viewport-relative.","fail":"The primary layout sizes/positions elements in pixel Offset (the engine default), or relies on a 'looks right on my monitor' build that does not re-proportion with the viewport."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing Size and Position driven by UDim2 Scale (fraction of parent) for the main layout, with Offset confined to genuinely non-scaling elements (stroke thickness, fixed small icons). A device-emulator screenshot across phone/tablet/desktop with the layout holding proportions is credited toward full marks but not required at the design-claim stage. Pixel-Offset positions/sizes on the primary layout, or a 'looks right on my monitor' build, do not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Convert every layout Size/Position from Offset to Scale (e.g. via AutoScaleLite or by hand), leaving Offset only on UIStroke thickness and crisp small icons; then verify the layout holds in the Studio Device Emulator across phone, tablet, and desktop.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/ui/position-and-size","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/designing-ui-tips-and-best-practices/3074034","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZTzUHfMeYKA"]}},{"id":"gui-anchorpoint-intent","order_index":2,"tier":"baseline","system_module":"gui","statement":"Every element's AnchorPoint is set to match its on-screen role BEFORE positioning, so the element scales and re-positions from the correct origin instead of drifting at the default 0,0. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to deliberate per-element anchoring tied to screen location: passing requires the build to state AnchorPoint chosen by role — centered panels at (0.5,0.5) with Position fromScale(0.5,…), corner HUD elements at the matching corner (1,1 bottom-right currency, 0,0 top-left label) — paired with Scale positions. A build that leaves frames at the default AnchorPoint 0,0 and positions by Offset, so two 'aligned' elements drift apart across aspect ratios, FAILS. A build that commits to role-matched AnchorPoint paired with Scale position passes.","above_generic_rationale":"Default frames anchor at 0,0 and shift unpredictably across aspect ratios — invisible when right, glaringly wrong (overlap/cropping/drift) when ignored, and it is the subtle variable two independent creators name as why 'aligned' labels stop lining up per device. Setting AnchorPoint to the element's screen origin is the deliberate move that makes scaled UI stay put; grading the role-matched-anchor commitment keeps this discriminating against the leave-it-at-0,0 default.","grading":{"pass":"AnchorPoint is assigned per element by screen role (0.5,0.5 for centered panels; matching corner for HUD corners) and paired with Scale-based Position, set before positioning, so elements grow from the correct origin and stay aligned across aspect ratios.","partial":"AnchorPoint is set deliberately on some elements but others are left at the default 0,0, OR role-matched anchors are stated but positions still use Offset, so alignment holds only partially across aspect ratios.","fail":"Elements rely on the default 0,0 AnchorPoint with Offset positioning, so nominally 'aligned' elements drift apart, overlap, or crop across aspect ratios."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing AnchorPoint assigned per element by screen role (0.5,0.5 for centered panels; matching corner for HUD corners) and paired with Scale-based Position, set before positioning. Reliance on the default 0,0 anchor with Offset positioning, or two elements that drift apart across aspect ratios, does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"For each element set AnchorPoint to its screen origin first (0.5,0.5 centered panel, 1,1 bottom-right currency, 0,0 top-left label), then position with Scale so it grows from that origin and never drifts when the screen resizes.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/designing-ui-tips-and-best-practices/3074034","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BLZl-0cl2DM"]}},{"id":"gui-layout-driven-composition","order_index":3,"tier":"baseline","system_module":"gui","statement":"KILLER SYSTEM — LAYOUT-DRIVEN COMPOSITION. Repeated/structured arrangements (HUD rows, menus, shop grids, inventory cells, settings lists) are produced declaratively by layout objects — UIListLayout / UIGridLayout / UITableLayout — NOT by hand-positioning each sibling, so spacing stays uniform and the design survives adding/removing items. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to layout objects owning placement: passing requires the build to state that siblings are arranged by a layout object using its semantics — FillDirection, SortOrder=LayoutOrder with per-child LayoutOrder integers, and Padding for the gap BETWEEN items — rather than manual Position per element. A build that hand-positions menu/grid items (so adding one breaks alignment, the 'randomly placed ImageLabels' look) FAILS. A build that commits to UIListLayout/UIGridLayout/UITableLayout-driven placement passes at the architecture level; correct grid cell semantics (CellSize/CellPadding) and group alignment strengthen it.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic UI manually positions every item, so the spacing is haphazard and a single edit breaks the row — the 'elements all over the place' critique reviewers cite most. Layout objects guarantee the uniform rhythm that reads as systematic, and the LayoutOrder ordering / padding-is-between-items semantics make menus maintainable. Grading the layout-object commitment keeps this discriminating: a declaratively-laid-out build clears it, a hand-positioned build structurally cannot keep alignment under edits.","grading":{"pass":"Repeated/structured arrangements are driven by UIListLayout / UIGridLayout / UITableLayout using their semantics (FillDirection; SortOrder=LayoutOrder + per-child LayoutOrder; Padding between items; CellSize/CellPadding for grids), so spacing is uniform and the design survives adding/removing items.","partial":"A layout object is used for some arrangements but other repeated groups are still hand-positioned, OR a layout object is present but its semantics (LayoutOrder, Padding, CellSize) are not used deliberately, leaving alignment fragile under edits.","fail":"Menu/grid/list items are hand-positioned by manual Position per element, so adding or removing an item breaks alignment (the 'randomly placed ImageLabels' look)."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing repeated/structured arrangements driven by UIListLayout / UIGridLayout / UITableLayout using their semantics (FillDirection; SortOrder=LayoutOrder + per-child LayoutOrder; Padding between items; CellSize/CellPadding for grids). Manual per-element Position on lists/grids, or arrangement that breaks when an item is added/removed, does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Replace hand-positioned siblings with a layout object: UIListLayout for rows/columns (set FillDirection, SortOrder=LayoutOrder, Padding for inter-item gaps), UIGridLayout for inventory/shop cells (CellSize/CellPadding), UITableLayout for tabular rows — so spacing stays uniform and the design survives edits.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/ui/list-flex-layouts","https://create.roblox.com/docs/ui/grid-table-layouts","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/hud-design-feedback/3246212"]}},{"id":"gui-padding-breathing-room","order_index":4,"tier":"baseline","system_module":"gui","statement":"Internal spacing is systematic, not incidental: every container applies UIPadding so content never touches its frame edges, and inter-item gaps come from UIListLayout/UIGridLayout Padding — ideally one repeated padding value used everywhere — distinguishing inner margins (UIPadding) from between-item gutters (layout Padding). The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to systematic padding: passing requires the build to state UIPadding on containers (so text/icons have breathing room without resizing the container) AND consistent layout Padding for gutters, with a coherent (ideally single) spacing measurement rather than per-element guesses. A build whose content is jammed against frame borders with zero/inconsistent padding (the most common 'cramped / all over the place' reviewer note) FAILS. A build that commits to systematic UIPadding + layout Padding passes; using whitespace as a deliberate emphasis tool strengthens it.","above_generic_rationale":"The single most common generic-UI failure is content jammed against borders with cramped, inconsistent gaps; a single repeated padding value is what reads as 'systematic' versus 'haphazard'. Separating internal margins (UIPadding) from between-item gutters (layout Padding), and treating whitespace as an emphasis lever, is the difference between 'cramped placeholder' and 'designed'. Grading the systematic-padding commitment keeps this discriminating against a no-padding build.","grading":{"pass":"UIPadding is applied on containers (inner margins) and consistent UIListLayout/UIGridLayout Padding governs between-item gutters, ideally one repeated padding measurement, so content never sits flush to frame edges. Using extra whitespace as a deliberate emphasis lever strengthens it.","partial":"Some padding is present but it is inconsistent across containers, OR inner margins and between-item gutters are not distinguished, so spacing reads as partly ad-hoc rather than a single systematic measure.","fail":"Content is jammed against frame borders with zero or inconsistent padding (the 'cramped / all over the place' note), with spacing picked per element rather than systematically."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing UIPadding applied on containers (inner margins) and consistent UIListLayout/UIGridLayout Padding (between-item gutters), ideally one repeated padding measurement, with content never flush to frame edges. Per-element ad-hoc spacing or content touching borders does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Apply a consistent UIPadding inside every card/button/list container so nothing touches the edges, set inter-item gaps via UIListLayout/UIGridLayout Padding (not hand spacing), reuse one padding value across the UI, and use extra whitespace around an element to mark it as important.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/a-guide-to-user-interface-design/47526","https://create.roblox.com/docs/ui/appearance-modifiers","https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/game-design/ui-ux-design"]}},{"id":"gui-role-based-palette","order_index":5,"tier":"baseline","system_module":"gui","statement":"Color is governed by a constrained, ROLE-based palette defined once — ~4-5 intentional colors with a single accent reserved for the call-to-action, each color assigned a role (base, outline, text, background-2, accent) and stored centrally (e.g. a Configuration object) so the whole UI is re-skinnable — and primary vs secondary actions are differentiated (primary = filled accent button; secondary = muted/gray/text-only). The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to a central role-based palette with action differentiation: passing requires the build to state (1) a constrained palette (~4-5 colors, one accent for CTA) with colors assigned roles and reused everywhere rather than picked ad-hoc per element, AND (2) primary/secondary button distinction so not every button is identically loud. A build with rainbow/ad-hoc per-element color, or a wall of identical bright buttons, FAILS. A build that commits to a central role-palette + primary/secondary differentiation passes.","above_generic_rationale":"Rainbow, ad-hoc color is the loudest amateur signal and a wall of identical bright buttons (classic simulator look) causes misclicks. A constrained role-based palette with one accent is what makes the accent actually mean 'click here', and storing roles centrally makes the game re-skinnable from one place. Grading the central-role-palette + primary/secondary commitment keeps this discriminating against ad-hoc per-element color.","grading":{"pass":"A constrained palette (~4-5 colors, one reserved accent for CTAs) has colors assigned roles and reused across elements (ideally stored in a Configuration/central source), AND primary buttons are filled-accent while secondary buttons are muted/gray/text-only.","partial":"A constrained palette is used but roles are not centralized/named, OR the palette is disciplined but primary/secondary actions are not visually differentiated (every button equally loud).","fail":"Color is ad-hoc per element (rainbow), or the UI is a wall of identical bright buttons with no role assignment and no primary/secondary distinction."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing a constrained palette (~4-5 colors, one reserved accent) with colors assigned roles and reused across elements (ideally stored in a Configuration/central source), plus a primary-vs-secondary button distinction (filled accent vs muted/text). Ad-hoc per-element color or uniformly loud buttons do not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Define ~4-5 role-named colors up front (base, outline, text, background-2, one accent for CTAs) in a central Configuration, reuse those roles everywhere instead of picking colors per element, and make primary buttons filled-accent while secondary buttons are muted/gray or text-only.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/the-ultimate-ui-design-guide/1236916","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZTzUHfMeYKA","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-to-make-extremely-clean-ui-while-keeping-good-ux/2438099"]}},{"id":"gui-fullscreen-container-architecture","order_index":6,"tier":"baseline","system_module":"gui","statement":"KILLER SYSTEM — GOVERNED RESPONSIVE LAYER. The interface is reasoned about as ONE governed responsive layer whose scaling/anchoring is owned in a single place, not a pile of independently-placed elements: the build commits to a unifying structure (a transparent full-screen root frame, OR a stated responsive-layout system that holds the whole subtree's proportions across devices) so children inherit the layer's responsive behavior rather than each carrying its own absolute position. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to one point of layout governance that re-proportions as a unit: passing requires the build to state that the whole UI lives under / is driven by a deliberate responsive container or scale-based layout system that owns placement/scaling for the layer (so it 'holds on phone/tablet/desktop' as one governed unit), NOT elements each hand-placed by absolute position. A build that describes elements 'hand-placed by absolute position' / scattered with no governing responsive layer FAILS. A build that commits to a single governed responsive layer (root frame, or a stated scale-based layout system holding the subtree's proportions as a unit) passes; naming ResetOnSpawn/DisplayOrder handling strengthens it toward full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic builds dump elements at absolute positions ('all over the place') and never make the one structural decision that governs the layer as a unit (the MEAN). Committing to a single responsive container/layer that owns the subtree's scaling is exactly the architectural intent a quality build expresses and a hand-placed build structurally cannot — discriminating at design-claim altitude without demanding a property dump the claim omits.","grading":{"pass":"The whole UI is governed as one responsive layer — a single deliberate top-level root frame, OR a stated scale-based layout system that owns the subtree's placement/scaling and holds proportions across devices as a unit — so children inherit the layer's responsive behavior; ResetOnSpawn/DisplayOrder handling strengthens it.","partial":"A governing/responsive layer is committed for part of the UI but some elements are still described as independently/absolutely placed, OR a unified layer is implied without stating the root or the scale-based layout system that owns it.","fail":"Elements are hand-placed by absolute position or scattered across the layer with no governing container/root and no scale-based layout system owning the subtree."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing the whole UI governed as one responsive layer — a single transparent full-screen root frame, OR a stated scale-based layout system that owns the subtree's placement/scaling and holds proportions across phone/tablet/desktop as a unit — so children inherit the layer's responsive behavior rather than each carrying an absolute position. ResetOnSpawn/DisplayOrder handling is credited toward full marks. Elements hand-placed by absolute position or scattered with no governing responsive layer does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Govern the whole UI as one responsive layer: wrap it in a single transparent full-screen root frame (or commit to a scale-based layout system that owns the subtree's scaling) so the layer is positioned/scaled once and children inherit it across phone/tablet/desktop, instead of giving each element its own absolute position; add ResetOnSpawn=false / DisplayOrder for HUD persistence.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/ui-looks-very-bad/2614669","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BLZl-0cl2DM"]},"change_reason":"Old bar demanded a literal transparent root frame plus an explicit ResetOnSpawn/DisplayOrder dump, granularity the GREAT design-claim never states — so it scored fail alongside the MEAN, zero discrimination. The GREAT DOES commit to one governed responsive layer ('fully responsive scale-based layout … that holds on phone/tablet/desktop' + 'reusable component templates' = a unified system that re-proportions as a unit), while the MEAN explicitly says elements are 'hand-placed by absolute position … no AnchorPoint'. Re-anchoring pass to 'a single governing container OR a stated scale-based layout system that owns subtree scaling as a unit' (the systems-intent the GREAT expresses) and pinning fail to the MEAN's stated 'hand-placed/scattered absolute position' restores discrimination; ResetOnSpawn/DisplayOrder specifics become the strengthener.","change_reason_dup_guard":"n/a"},{"id":"gui-aspect-and-size-constraints","order_index":7,"tier":"good","system_module":"gui","statement":"Proportions and pixel extremes are governed by constraint objects placed at the CONTAINER level — UIAspectRatioConstraint locks card/panel shape, UISizeConstraint (Min/MaxSize) clamps elements, UITextSizeConstraint keeps text legible — so Scale-sized UI never stretches into distorted shapes or becomes untappable/absurd on extreme aspect ratios (tablets, ultrawide, 4K). The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to container-level constraints rather than scripting device checks or over-constraining every child: passing requires the build to state (1) a UIAspectRatioConstraint on the governing frame (NOT on every child) to hold proportions, AND (2) at least one clamp (UISizeConstraint Min/Max or UITextSizeConstraint, never below ~9px) protecting usability at the extremes. A build that relies on Scale alone with no aspect/size constraints — so frames skew on iPads/ultrawide and buttons get untappable on phones — FAILS. A build that commits to container-level aspect + size/text clamps passes; the documented 'constraints override UIListLayout-controlled sizing' awareness and scaled UICorner radius strengthen it.","above_generic_rationale":"Pure Scale still distorts non-proportionally on portrait/ultrawide and lets buttons become untappable on small screens or absurd on 4K — the gap the generic build ignores. Constraints are the documented, zero-code companions to Scale; putting them at the container level (one constraint governs the whole subtree) rather than on every child is the clean architecture pros use, and over-constraining every image button produces a 'big mess'. Grading the container-level-constraint commitment keeps this discriminating against a Scale-only build.","grading":{"pass":"A UIAspectRatioConstraint sits on the controlling frame (not duplicated onto every child) holding proportions, PLUS at least one clamp — UISizeConstraint Min/MaxSize or UITextSizeConstraint with a stated floor (~9px) — protecting tiny-phone and large-monitor usability. Awareness that constraints override UIListLayout sizing and scaled UICorner radius strengthen it.","partial":"An aspect-ratio constraint OR a size/text clamp is present but not both, OR a constraint is stamped on every child rather than at the governing container level, so coverage of the extremes is partial.","fail":"The build relies on Scale alone with no aspect/size/text constraints, so frames skew on tablets/ultrawide and buttons become untappable on phones or absurd on 4K."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing a UIAspectRatioConstraint on the controlling frame (not duplicated onto every child) plus at least one clamp — UISizeConstraint Min/MaxSize or UITextSizeConstraint with a stated floor (~9px) — protecting tiny-phone and large-monitor usability. Awareness that these constraints override UIListLayout-controlled sizing, and scaled (not fixed-Offset) UICorner radius, are credited toward full marks. Scale-only sizing with no constraints, or a constraint stamped on every child, does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Put one UIAspectRatioConstraint on each governing panel/card frame to hold its proportions, add UISizeConstraint Min/MaxSize and UITextSizeConstraint (min ~9px) to clamp the extremes, drive UICorner radius with Scale not Offset, and remember these constraints override UIListLayout sizing — so place them deliberately.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/ui/size-modifiers.md","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/designing-ui-tips-and-best-practices/3074034","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kuWl_t1AOE","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BLZl-0cl2DM"]}},{"id":"gui-text-and-content-driven-sizing","order_index":8,"tier":"good","system_module":"gui","statement":"Text scales WITH its container instead of being a fixed pixel size on a scaling layout: the build commits to text that re-sizes proportionally as the viewport/container changes (TextScaled, a text-size constraint pairing, or an equivalent stated mechanism) AND/OR content boxes that grow to fit variable/localized strings rather than hard-coded rectangles. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to text/content sizing that participates in the responsive system: passing requires the build to state that text does not stay one frozen size while the rest of the layout scales (the classic 'everything scales except the text' bug) — i.e. text sizing is tied to the responsive/type system, not a single fixed TextSize. A build that states 'one default font size' on fixed-offset elements FAILS. A build whose responsive/type-scale commitment explicitly carries text sizing with the layout (text scales with container, or boxes auto-fit content) passes; naming TextScaled/AutomaticSize with the Size-is-minimum / AnchorPoint-pivot detail strengthens it.","above_generic_rationale":"The #1 device-correctness tell is text that stays one frozen size while everything else scales; the MEAN's 'one default font size' is exactly that floor. A quality build commits to text that participates in the responsive system (scales with container, boxes auto-fit content). Grading that commitment — rather than demanding the exact API token the design-claim omits — keeps it discriminating between a responsive type system and a fixed-size build.","grading":{"pass":"Text resizes with its container / the responsive system (TextScaled, a text-size-constraint pairing, or an explicit text-scales-with-layout commitment) AND/OR content boxes auto-fit variable/localized strings. Naming TextScaled/AutomaticSize with Size-as-minimum / AnchorPoint-pivot awareness strengthens it.","partial":"A type scale is committed but it is not stated that text scales with the layout (or only one of text-scaling vs content-auto-fit is addressed where both are needed), so content-driven sizing is only partly committed.","fail":"Text is one fixed/default size on a scaling or fixed-offset layout, or boxes are hard-coded so longer/localized strings would clip."},"evidence_required":"A statement that text resizes with its container / the responsive system rather than holding one fixed pixel size while the layout scales — via TextScaled, a text-size-constraint pairing, or an explicit 'text scales with the layout' commitment — AND/OR content boxes that grow to fit variable/localized strings. Naming TextScaled and/or AutomaticSize (Size acts as minimum, AnchorPoint as pivot) is credited toward full marks. 'One default font size' on fixed elements, or a type scale with no statement that text scales with the layout, does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Tie text sizing into the responsive system: enable TextScaled (clamped by UITextSizeConstraint) so labels resize with their container, and set AutomaticSize on boxes holding variable/localized text so they grow to fit — instead of one fixed font size frozen on a scaling layout.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/ui/size-modifiers.md","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BLZl-0cl2DM","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_q4fcM1eWvg"]},"change_reason":"Old bar required a literal mention of TextScaled or AutomaticSize, which the GREAT design-claim omitted (it said 'consistent type scale' but not the API), so it failed the GREAT and tied the MEAN. The MEAN explicitly states 'one default font size' on fixed-offset elements — the precise frozen-text fail. Re-anchoring the pass to 'text sizing is part of the responsive/type system (scales with container / auto-fits content)' lets the GREAT's responsive + type-scale commitment clear it while the MEAN's single-default-size build cannot, restoring discrimination; the specific API names become a strengthener, not the gate."},{"id":"gui-auto-canvas-scrolling","order_index":9,"tier":"good","system_module":"gui","statement":"Lists/grids that can hold a variable number of items (inventories, leaderboards, shop rows, settings) are rendered by a content-measuring system whose extent is DERIVED from the items, not hand-placed in a fixed area: the build commits to a scrolling/list region driven by a layout object + reusable item template so the canvas (or list height) grows and shrinks with the item set and survives add/remove, with scrollbar inset handled so content isn't hidden under the bar. The discriminator is whether placement of repeated items is OWNED by a layout/template system that measures content (so adding an item just reflows) versus each item carrying its own absolute Position: passing requires the build to state that scrollable/long item sets are produced by a UIListLayout/UIGridLayout-driven, component-template list whose canvas is content-derived (AutomaticCanvasSize, a CanvasSize-to-AbsoluteContentSize binding, OR an explicit 'the canvas fits the items and survives add/remove' commitment), NOT plain frames hand-placed by absolute position. A build of plain frames 'hand-placed by absolute position' with no layout/scroll system FAILS. A build whose repeated items are layout-driven with a content-fitted canvas passes; naming AutomaticCanvasSize/AbsoluteContentSize, VerticalScrollBarInset, and the Offset-vs-Scale-padding gotcha strengthens it toward full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"The generic build hand-places items in a fixed area (the MEAN's absolute-positioned plain frames), so the first time the item count changes the list breaks — exactly the floor. A layout-driven, template-cloned list whose canvas is measured from content is the system that survives dynamic data, and it is precisely the layout-and-template substrate a quality build commits to ('UIListLayout/UIGridLayout-driven ... reusable component templates') and a hand-placed-absolute build structurally cannot. Grading the content-measured-list commitment keeps this discriminating at design-claim altitude without demanding a property dump the claim omits.","grading":{"pass":"Repeated/variable-length items are placed by a layout object + reusable item template and the scroll extent is content-derived (AutomaticCanvasSize, CanvasSize↔AbsoluteContentSize binding, or an explicit 'canvas fits items and survives add/remove' commitment) with scrollbar inset handled. Naming the inset/gotcha/ElasticBehavior strengthens it.","partial":"A layout-driven/templated list is committed but the content-derived canvas or scrollbar inset is unaddressed (e.g. layout present but the canvas is hard-set, or inset ignored), so robustness on count change is only partly committed.","fail":"List/grid items are hand-placed by absolute Position with no layout/scroll system, or the canvas is hard-set so it breaks when the item count changes."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing repeated/variable-length items produced by a layout object + reusable item template whose scroll extent is content-derived — AutomaticCanvasSize, a CanvasSize-to-AbsoluteContentSize binding, OR an explicit 'canvas fits the items and survives add/remove' commitment — with scrollbar inset handled. Naming AutomaticCanvasSize/AbsoluteContentSize, VerticalScrollBarInset, and the Offset-vs-Scale-padding gotcha is credited toward full marks. Plain frames hand-placed by absolute Position with no layout/scroll system, or a hard-set canvas that breaks on count change, does NOT satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Render variable-length lists/grids from a layout object (UIListLayout/UIGridLayout) cloning a reusable item template, and make the scroll extent content-derived: set AutomaticCanvasSize (or bind CanvasSize to the layout's AbsoluteContentSize) so the canvas fits the items and survives add/remove, set VerticalScrollBarInset so nothing hides under the bar, and use Offset (not Scale) layout padding so the auto-size stays correct — instead of hand-placing items by absolute Position in a fixed frame.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/ui/scrolling-frames","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kuWl_t1AOE"]},"change_reason":"Old bar gated the pass on the literal AutomaticCanvasSize/AbsoluteContentSize/VerticalScrollBarInset tokens, which the GREAT design-claim never names — so both the GREAT and the MEAN scored fail, zero discrimination. But the GREAT explicitly commits to 'a clean UIListLayout/UIGridLayout-driven structure with reusable component templates instead of hand-positioned offsets' — that IS a content-measuring, layout-owned list substrate from which a content-derived (auto-fitted) scroll canvas follows directly; whereas the MEAN explicitly 'hand-places plain frames by absolute position' with no layout or scroll system, the exact build that breaks the moment the item count changes. Re-anchoring the pass to 'repeated items rendered by a layout/component-template system with a content-derived extent that survives add/remove' (the systems-level capability the GREAT's stated substrate genuinely expresses) lets the GREAT clear it while the MEAN's hand-placed-absolute build cannot, restoring discrimination; the specific scrolling APIs become strengtheners, not the gate."},{"id":"gui-page-layout-multiscreen","order_index":10,"tier":"good","system_module":"gui","statement":"Switching between panels/screens/tabs (settings categories, shop pages, menu views) is handled by a SYSTEMATIZED, ANIMATED mechanism — reusable templated panels whose open/close/switch is one tweened operation — rather than a single static screen or hand-toggled Visible flags that blink. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to maintainable, animated multi-view management: passing requires the build to state that views/panels are switched through a systematized mechanism (a paged/tab system, OR reusable component-template panels) WITH animated open/close/switch transitions, so adding a view doesn't mean more hand-wired toggling and screens don't blink in/out. A build of a single static hand-placed screen with 'no transitions' and no navigation/switching system FAILS. A build that commits to systematized, animated panel/screen switching (templated panels + tweened open/close/switch) passes; naming UIPageLayout with EasingStyle/Direction/TweenTime and the inner-padding centering gotcha strengthens it toward full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"Beginners ship a single static screen or hand-rolled Visible-toggling that blinks (the MEAN); a systematized, animated panel/screen-switching mechanism is the maintainable way to do multi-view menus and the felt difference between 'made in 10 minutes' and 'shipped'. That systematized-and-animated intent is exactly what the GREAT's reusable-templates + tweened-transitions commitment expresses and what the MEAN's static, transition-less single screen structurally lacks. Grading the systematized-animated-switching commitment keeps this discriminating without demanding the exact API the design-claim omits.","grading":{"pass":"View/panel/tab switching uses a systematized mechanism (UIPageLayout OR reusable component-template panels) WITH animated open/close/switch transitions — one switch operation, not a lone static screen or hand-toggled blink. Naming UIPageLayout transition config and the centering-padding gotcha strengthens it.","partial":"Animated panel transitions and reusable templates are committed (implying systematized switching) but no explicit multi-view/tab/page switching is named, OR a paged system is named but transitions are left at defaults / the centering gotcha is unaddressed.","fail":"Navigation is a single static screen with no system and no transitions, or views/tabs switch by hand-toggled Visible flags that blink in/out."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing view/panel/tab switching via a systematized mechanism (UIPageLayout, OR reusable component-template panels) WITH animated open/close/switch transitions, so a switch is one systematized animated operation rather than a lone static screen or hand-toggled blink. Naming UIPageLayout with EasingStyle/Direction/TweenTime and the inner-padding centering gotcha is credited toward full marks. A single static screen with no transitions/navigation, or instant Visible-toggling that blinks, does NOT satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Drive panel/screen/tab switches through a systematized mechanism — a UIPageLayout, or reusable templated panels — where a single switch operation animates the outgoing view out and the incoming view in (set EasingStyle/Direction/TweenTime, add inner UIPadding so pages center without the next peeking) — instead of shipping one static screen or hand-toggling each view's Visible flag with no transition.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"medium","sources":["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kuWl_t1AOE"]},"change_reason":"Old bar required the literal UIPageLayout API plus its centering-padding gotcha; the GREAT design-claim names neither, so both the GREAT and MEAN failed — zero discrimination. The GREAT commits to 'reusable component templates' AND 'tweened open/close/hover transitions (juice)', which together are exactly a systematized, animated panel/screen-switching mechanism (templated views that animate in/out), whereas the MEAN is explicitly a single static hand-placed screen with 'no transitions' and no navigation. Re-anchoring the pass to 'systematized animated multi-view switching (templated panels + tweened open/close/switch, one operation — not a lone static screen or blink toggles)' lets the GREAT's template + tweened-transition commitment clear it while the MEAN's static, transition-less single screen fails; UIPageLayout specifics become strengtheners. This is the systems intent the GREAT expresses, surfaced honestly rather than gated on an API token it omits."},{"id":"gui-typographic-hierarchy","order_index":11,"tier":"good","system_module":"gui","statement":"Type establishes a clear header/subhead/body hierarchy through SIZE and WEIGHT within a single clean non-decorative font family (Gotham in-engine; Montserrat/Quicksand analogs), with secondary text de-emphasized by subtle TextTransparency (~0.1) rather than shrunk into illegibility, and labels/values aligned deliberately within rows (label-left, value-right). The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to a disciplined type scale in one family: passing requires the build to state (1) a 2-3 tier size+weight scale (bold headers carrying scannable info, lighter body) kept to ONE family, AND (2) row alignment/de-emphasis discipline (label-left/value-right, transparency for secondary, never the default Legacy font or mixed decorative fonts). A build with one undifferentiated text size everywhere, or mixed/decorative/Legacy fonts, FAILS. A build that commits to a one-family weighted type scale with aligned rows passes.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic UI uses one undifferentiated text size or mixes clashing/Legacy fonts (an instant amateur tell), and left-stacks every row. A disciplined 3-tier type scale in a single family is the cheapest, highest-impact upgrade that makes a screen instantly scannable, and label-left/value-right alignment is the recognized clean-row pattern. Grading the one-family-weighted-scale commitment keeps this discriminating against flat-type, mixed-font builds.","grading":{"pass":"A header/subhead/body size+weight scale lives within ONE clean non-decorative family (no Legacy/mixed decorative fonts), with deliberate row alignment (labels left, values right) and TextTransparency (~0.1) de-emphasizing secondary text rather than shrinking it.","partial":"A size+weight scale in one family is present but row alignment / secondary de-emphasis discipline is missing, OR alignment is handled but type is only weakly differentiated, so hierarchy is partial.","fail":"One undifferentiated text size is used everywhere, or fonts are mixed/decorative/Legacy, so there is no scannable type hierarchy."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing a header/subhead/body size+weight scale within ONE clean non-decorative family (no Legacy/mixed decorative fonts), plus deliberate row alignment (labels left, values right) and TextTransparency (~0.1) to de-emphasize secondary text rather than shrinking it. One uniform text size, or mixed/decorative fonts, does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Pick one clean font family and build a 3-tier scale by varying size and weight (bold headers, lighter body), keep to 2-3 text colors, left-align labels and right-align values in rows, and de-emphasize secondary text with ~0.1 TextTransparency instead of shrinking it.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/ui-design-and-the-fundamentals/1455805","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-to-make-extremely-clean-ui-while-keeping-good-ux/2438099","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/heres-how-to-create-a-modern-sleek-ui-design-ui-tutorial/2528850"]}},{"id":"gui-semantic-color-consistency","order_index":12,"tier":"good","system_module":"gui","statement":"Color is governed by a single defined-once palette REUSED systematically across the whole UI, ideally with meaning-bearing concepts (Health, currency, rarity) locked to fixed colors repeated on their text/icon/bar wherever they appear — color is a consistency system, not a per-screen aesthetic choice or an engine default. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to a defined-once, reused color system (and at full strength, concept-to-color binding) versus shipping engine-default gray/white with no color system: passing requires the build to state a constrained palette defined once and reused as a system across the UI (a single source of color truth), with the strongest builds binding each concept to one color reused identically across surfaces. A build of 'default gray/white colors' with NO color system at all FAILS. A build that commits to a constrained, defined-once palette reused systematically across the UI passes; explicit concept-to-color binding reused across text/icon/bar strengthens it toward full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic UI ships gray defaults or colors per-screen aesthetics (the MEAN's 'default gray/white'), so color carries no consistency. A defined-once palette reused as a system across surfaces — and at full strength concept-locked color repeated on text+icon+bar — is a systems-level consistency a default-gray build never reaches. Grading the defined-once-reused-system commitment keeps it discriminating while leaving a bare aesthetic claim at partial.","grading":{"pass":"A constrained palette is defined once and reused systematically across the UI (single source of color truth, ideally with a reserved accent role), so color is a consistency system rather than a per-screen choice or default. Concept-to-color binding reused across text/icon/bar strengthens it toward full marks.","partial":"A constrained/intentional palette is committed but it is not stated to be defined-once/centralized and reused as a system, OR a defined-once palette is present but concepts are not locked to fixed colors reused across text+icon+bar, so semantic reuse is only partial.","fail":"Default gray/white or per-screen ad-hoc color with no defined-once reused palette and no concept-to-color binding at all."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing a constrained palette defined once and reused systematically across the UI (a single source of color truth — e.g. a central palette/Configuration with a reserved accent), ideally with concepts bound to fixed colors reused identically across their text/icon/bar on every surface. 'Default gray/white' or per-screen ad-hoc color with no defined-once reused palette does not satisfy this; concept-to-color binding is credited toward full marks.","concrete_fix":"Define a constrained palette once (a central palette/Configuration with a reserved accent) and reuse those colors systematically across every surface instead of shipping engine-default gray/white; for full marks, lock each meaning-bearing concept (Health, each currency/rarity) to one fixed color and repeat it on that concept's text, icon, AND bar everywhere it appears.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"medium","sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/game-design/ui-ux-design"]},"change_reason":"Old bar required explicit per-concept bindings (Health=green across text+icon+bar) the GREAT design-claim didn't enumerate (it stated a 'constrained palette' with 'a single accent reserved for the call-to-action'), so the GREAT scored fail and tied the MEAN's 'default gray/white'. The honest gap: the MEAN has NO color system (reflex gray default), while the GREAT commits to a defined-once constrained palette reused as a system — a real consistency commitment, even if it doesn't enumerate per-concept bindings. Re-anchoring pass to 'a defined-once constrained palette reused systematically across the UI' lets the GREAT's stated palette-system clear it while the MEAN's no-color-system default fails; concept-to-color binding remains the strengthener for full marks, preserving sharpness above a bare aesthetic claim."},{"id":"gui-theme-to-mood-match","order_index":13,"tier":"good","system_module":"gui","statement":"The palette/mood is a deliberate, considered visual choice rather than an engine reflex default: the build commits to an intentional, quality-considered aesthetic (ideally matched to this experience's genre/tone — playful sim → bright/high-chroma; serious/competitive → restrained dark) instead of shipping engine-default gray/white or a thoughtless look. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to a deliberate, considered visual mood versus a reflex default: passing requires the build to state that the palette/mood is an intentional design decision (a constrained, quality-aimed aesthetic), with the strongest builds tying that choice explicitly to the game's genre/tone. A build that ships 'default gray/white colors' as an unconsidered reflex default FAILS. A build that commits to a deliberately chosen, quality-considered palette/mood passes; explicit genre→palette reasoning strengthens it toward full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic builds ship engine defaults (the MEAN's gray) with no considered aesthetic. Committing to a deliberate, quality-considered palette/mood (ideally genre-matched) is a higher-order judgment that signals the UI was made FOR this game. Grading the 'deliberate considered decision vs reflex default' axis keeps it discriminating between the GREAT's intentional constrained aesthetic and the reflex default the MEAN embodies.","grading":{"pass":"The palette/mood is committed as a deliberate, quality-considered decision (a constrained, intentional aesthetic, not a default); explicit genre→palette reasoning strengthens it toward full marks.","partial":"A considered/non-default aesthetic is committed but the tone/genre-fit reasoning is implicit, so the deliberate-mood-match is only weakly evidenced.","fail":"Engine-default gray/white (or a generic look applied regardless of game) shipped as a reflex with no deliberate visual decision."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing the palette/mood was a deliberate, quality-considered design decision (a constrained, intentional aesthetic), ideally tied to this experience's genre/tone, not an engine/gray reflex default or a generic look applied regardless of game. Explicit genre→palette reasoning is credited toward full marks. 'Default gray/white colors' applied as a reflex does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Treat the palette/mood as a deliberate decision: commit to a constrained, intentional aesthetic and (for full marks) state the game's emotional tone first, then pull the palette to match it (bright/high-chroma for playful sims, restrained dark for serious/competitive) — rather than shipping engine-default gray/white regardless of the game.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"medium","sources":["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZTzUHfMeYKA","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/the-ultimate-ui-design-guide/1236916"]},"change_reason":"Old bar required explicit genre→palette reasoning the GREAT design-claim left implicit, so the GREAT failed and tied the MEAN's gray default. The GREAT's 'award-quality … constrained palette … clear visual hierarchy' IS a deliberate, considered aesthetic decision, while the MEAN's 'default gray/white colors' is the literal unconsidered-reflex-default fail. Re-anchoring pass to 'a deliberately chosen, quality-considered palette/mood (a decision, not a default)' lets the GREAT's intentional-aesthetic commitment clear it while the MEAN's reflex default fails; fully-explicit genre→palette reasoning remains the strengthener for full marks, keeping a bare 'looks pro' claim at partial."},{"id":"gui-layered-depth-material","order_index":14,"tier":"good","system_module":"gui","statement":"Surfaces have authored DEPTH instead of being flat single-color rectangles: the build commits to layering that gives panels/buttons perceived material — a restrained combination of border (UIStroke), soft drop shadow, and/or subtle gradient, plus rounded corners — rather than flat fills with square corners. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to depth/material over flat rectangles: passing requires the build to state at least one deliberate depth treatment (stroke and/or shadow and/or gradient) AND rounded corners giving surfaces material, instead of the flat-frame default. A build of 'plain Frames' with 'square corners' and flat 'default gray/white' fills FAILS. A build that commits to layered depth (corner radius + at least one of stroke/shadow/gradient) passes; the full stroke+shadow+gradient stack (and avoiding icon-shadows / bare-text-strokes) strengthens it toward full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"Flat single-color frames with square corners are the clearest 'AI did it' surface (and exactly the MEAN). Committing to rounded corners plus a depth treatment is the cheapest high-impact move separating 'looks designed' from 'looks placed'. Grading the corner-radius + at-least-one-depth-layer commitment keeps it discriminating between a material build and the MEAN's flat default, without requiring the full property dump the design-claim omits.","grading":{"pass":"Rounded corners plus at least one deliberate depth treatment (UIStroke and/or soft drop shadow and/or subtle UIGradient) give surfaces perceived material. The full restrained stroke+shadow+gradient stack, and avoiding icon-shadows / bare-text-strokes, strengthens it.","partial":"Rounded corners are committed but depth treatment is thin or only implied (e.g. corner radius alone, or a single light gradient), so material is only partly authored.","fail":"Flat single-color frames with square corners and no depth treatment."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing rounded corners PLUS at least one deliberate depth treatment (UIStroke and/or soft drop shadow and/or subtle UIGradient) giving surfaces perceived material. The full restrained stroke+shadow+gradient stack — and explicitly avoiding shadows-on-icons / strokes-on-bare-text — is credited toward full marks. Flat single-color frames with square corners do not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Give panels/buttons material: add UICorner for rounded corners and at least one depth layer — a thin semi-transparent UIStroke, a soft drop shadow behind (not on icons), or a subtle UIGradient — building toward the full restrained stack, instead of flat single-color frames with square corners.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-to-make-extremely-clean-ui-while-keeping-good-ux/2438099","https://create.roblox.com/docs/ui/appearance-modifiers","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/uistroke-x-uigradient-is-a-great-combo-heres-why/1463030","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/ui-looks-very-bad/2614669"]},"change_reason":"Old bar demanded the full stroke+shadow+gradient layering plus named amateur-move avoidance, none of which the GREAT design-claim spelled out (it stated 'consistent UICorner radius'), so the GREAT failed and tied the MEAN. The MEAN explicitly ships 'plain Frames' with 'square corners' — the flat-rectangle fail. Re-anchoring pass to 'corner radius + at least one depth treatment' lets the GREAT's UICorner + design-system commitment clear it while the MEAN's flat-square-corner build fails; the full layered stack and amateur-move avoidance become strengtheners, restoring discrimination."},{"id":"gui-custom-asset-shapes","order_index":15,"tier":"good","system_module":"gui","statement":"Focal surfaces escape the engine-default SQUARE Frame/TextButton rectangle through authored shaping, rather than every element being a plain default primitive: the build commits to non-default surface shape — custom transparent-PNG art via ImageLabel/ImageButton (paneled/glossy buttons, non-rectangular shapes, icons), AND/OR a deliberate shape-authoring system that rounds and styles surfaces (consistent UICorner radius applied as part of a design system) so surfaces read as authored rather than as raw inserted square frames. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to escaping the default square-rectangle primitive for its surfaces (via custom image art OR a committed rounded/styled shape system) versus building everything from raw square Frames/TextButtons: passing requires the build to state that focal surfaces are shaped beyond the engine default — custom image assets and/or a propagated rounded-corner/styled-shape system. A build of 'plain Frames and TextLabels' and 'static TextButtons' with 'square corners' — all engine-default square primitives — FAILS. A build that commits to authored surface shaping passes; custom transparent-PNG focal art (white-base recolored via ImageColor3/UIGradient, texture/glow overlays) strengthens it toward full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"Raw default Frames/TextButtons with square corners are the clearest 'AI did it' surface and exactly the MEAN; the cheapest high-impact escape from that floor is to shape surfaces beyond the default — custom focal art and/or a propagated rounded/styled-shape system. That authored-shaping commitment is what the GREAT's 'consistent UICorner radius' within 'a real design system' expresses, and what the MEAN's plain-square-primitive build structurally lacks. Grading the escape-the-default-square-primitive commitment keeps this discriminating, with custom transparent-PNG focal art as the full-marks strengthener.","grading":{"pass":"Focal surfaces are shaped beyond the engine default — custom transparent-PNG image art via ImageLabel/ImageButton AND/OR a committed propagated rounded/styled-shape system (consistent UICorner radius + styling across surfaces). Custom white-base ImageColor3/UIGradient art and texture/glow overlays strengthen it.","partial":"Surfaces are rounded/styled in places but most focal elements remain raw default square primitives, OR shaping is implied without a committed system, so the escape from the default primitive is only partial.","fail":"The build is entirely default Frame/TextButton/TextLabel primitives with square corners and no authored shaping (no custom art, no committed rounded/styled-shape system)."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing focal surfaces shaped beyond the engine default — custom transparent-PNG image assets via ImageLabel/ImageButton AND/OR a committed rounded/styled-shape system (consistent UICorner radius applied as part of a design system) — rather than all-default square Frames/TextLabels/TextButtons with square corners. Custom transparent-PNG focal art (white-base ImageColor3/UIGradient recoloring, texture/glow overlays) is credited toward full marks. An all-default, square-cornered primitive build does NOT satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Escape the raw square-rectangle default for focal surfaces: author key shapes/icons (glossy buttons, hexagon tiles, close/currency icons) as transparent PNGs applied via ImageLabel/ImageButton (white-base so ImageColor3/UIGradient recolors them per theme/state), AND/OR commit to a propagated rounded/styled-shape system (consistent UICorner radius + styling applied across surfaces) — instead of building the whole UI from plain square Frames and TextButtons.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZTzUHfMeYKA","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_q4fcM1eWvg","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p_T0V9Lg2Ns"]},"change_reason":"Old bar required custom transparent-PNG image art, which the GREAT design-claim genuinely never names — so the previous note (correctly, for THAT bar) said both artifacts share the absence and the criterion can't discriminate. But the discriminating axis the criterion is really about is 'does the build escape the engine-default square-rectangle primitive for its surfaces' — and on that axis the artifacts DIVERGE sharply: the MEAN explicitly ships 'plain Frames ... square corners ... static TextButtons' (raw default square primitives), while the GREAT explicitly commits to 'consistent UICorner radius' within 'a real design system' (an authored, propagated rounded/styled shape treatment). Widening the pass to 'authored surface shaping (custom image art OR a committed rounded/styled-shape system)' — not only transparent PNGs — lets the GREAT's UICorner/design-system shaping clear it while the MEAN's square default-primitive build fails, restoring discrimination; custom PNG focal art remains the strengthener for full marks. This honestly grades the escape-the-default-primitive capability rather than a single API."},{"id":"gui-unified-icon-set","order_index":16,"tier":"good","system_module":"gui","statement":"Repeated small visual elements (icons, indicators, status glyphs, button glyphs) are drawn as a UNIFIED family whose appearance is governed centrally — one consistent style with color driven by the design system's palette (ideally ImageColor3-tinted monochrome icons recolored by theme/state) — rather than mismatched, multicolor, or default ad-hoc elements with no unifying system. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to a centrally-governed, palette-driven unified treatment of its repeated visual elements versus shipping ad-hoc default-gray elements with no unifying system: passing requires the build to state that its repeated visual-element family is unified by the central design system / constrained palette (one consistent style, color driven from the palette), with the strongest builds using a monochrome icon family tinted via ImageColor3 across hover/selected/disabled. A build of 'default gray/white' elements with no palette system and no unifying treatment FAILS. A build that commits to a unified, palette/design-system-governed visual-element family passes; an explicit monochrome icon set with ImageColor3 theme/state recoloring strengthens it toward full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"Mismatched, default-gray, or ungoverned repeated elements read as unfinished (the MEAN's default gray/white with no system); a unified family whose color is driven centrally from the palette is quiet professionalism. That central-governance-and-unification commitment is exactly what the GREAT's 'constrained palette' + 'reusable component templates' + design system expresses, and what the MEAN's ad-hoc default-gray build structurally lacks. Grading the unified-and-palette-governed-element commitment keeps this discriminating, with a monochrome ImageColor3 icon family as the full-marks strengthener.","grading":{"pass":"Repeated visual elements form one unified family governed by the central design system / constrained palette (one consistent style, color driven from the palette; ideally monochrome icons with ImageColor3 by theme/state). State/theme recoloring across hover/selected/disabled strengthens it.","partial":"A unified element treatment is committed but its color isn't clearly palette-driven/centralized, OR a mostly-unified family has some mismatched elements, so unification is only partial.","fail":"Repeated elements are default-gray/white, mismatched multicolor, or ad-hoc with no unifying design-system/palette governance."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing repeated visual elements unified by the central design system / constrained palette — one consistent style with color driven from the palette (ideally a monochrome icon family with ImageColor3 driving color by theme/state) — rather than default-gray, mismatched, or ungoverned ad-hoc elements. State/theme-driven ImageColor3 recoloring across hover/selected/disabled is credited toward full marks. Default gray/white elements with no palette system, or mismatched multicolor elements, do NOT satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Govern your repeated visual elements as one family from the design system: pick a single consistent icon/glyph style and drive its color from the constrained palette (render icons as monochrome white silhouettes recolored via ImageColor3 so they stay on-palette and respond to hover/selected/disabled), defined once and reused across the UI — instead of shipping default-gray, mismatched, or ungoverned ad-hoc elements.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-to-make-extremely-clean-ui-while-keeping-good-ux/2438099","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/the-ultimate-ui-design-guide/1236916"]},"change_reason":"Old bar gated the pass on a literal monochrome icon set tinted via ImageColor3, which the GREAT design-claim never names — so the previous note said both artifacts share the absence and the criterion can't discriminate. The honest discriminating axis underneath is 'are repeated visual elements unified and palette-governed by a central system' — and on that axis the artifacts diverge: the MEAN ships 'default gray/white colors' with NO color/design system (ad-hoc, ungoverned), while the GREAT commits to 'a real design system with a constrained palette' and 'reusable component templates' (a central source governing element appearance and color). Widening the pass to 'a unified, centrally-palette-governed treatment of repeated visual elements' (not strictly icons-via-ImageColor3) lets the GREAT's constrained-palette + component-template system clear it while the MEAN's ungoverned default-gray elements fail, restoring discrimination; the monochrome-icon-ImageColor3 family remains the strengthener for full marks. This grades the unification-and-palette-governance capability the GREAT genuinely expresses rather than a single API the claim omits."},{"id":"gui-contrast-readability","order_index":17,"tier":"good","system_module":"gui","statement":"KILLER SYSTEM — READABILITY & CONTRAST. Text/background contrast is engineered for legibility and accessibility: off-black backgrounds (e.g. ~35,35,35) and off-white text (never pure #000/#FFF), contrast verified with a checker, and separation built on VALUE + SATURATION rather than hue alone (so it survives color blindness — ~1 in 12 men), never relying on red/green-only signaling. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to value/saturation-based, checked contrast: passing requires the build to state (1) off-black/off-white tones instead of pure black/white to cut eye strain, AND (2) contrast/separation built on value+saturation (not hue-only complementary pairs) with no colorblind-fragile red/green-only signaling. A build using pure-black panels / pure-white text, or hue-only 'orange on blue'-style contrast that collapses for colorblind users, FAILS. A build that commits to off-tone, value/saturation-driven, checked contrast passes.","above_generic_rationale":"Pure black-on-white causes eye strain and pure-black panels look cheap; hue-only contrast ('orange on blue') collapses for the ~1 in 12 men with color blindness and vibrates uncomfortably. Off-tones plus contrast engineered on the value/saturation axis (and softened pure black/white) is the senior accessibility-aware move that reads as premium rather than harsh/default. Grading the value/saturation + off-tone commitment keeps this discriminating against pure-black/white, hue-only builds.","grading":{"pass":"Off-black backgrounds and off-white text (no pure #000/#FFF) with contrast verified against a checker, AND element separation built on value+saturation rather than hue alone, with no red/green-only signaling.","partial":"Off-tones are used but contrast isn't verified, OR contrast is engineered but still leans on hue-only separation in places, so accessibility is only partly covered.","fail":"Pure-black panels / pure-white text are used, or contrast relies on hue-only complementary pairs ('orange on blue') that collapse for colorblind users."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing off-black backgrounds and off-white text (no pure #000/#FFF), contrast verified against a checker, and element separation built on value+saturation rather than hue alone, with no red/green-only signaling. Pure-black/white text-on-panel, or hue-only complementary contrast, does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Use off-black backgrounds (~35,35,35) and off-white text instead of pure black/white, verify text/background contrast with a checker, and build separation on value (lightness) and saturation rather than hue alone — never relying on red/green-only signaling — so the UI stays legible for colorblind players.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-to-make-extremely-clean-ui-while-keeping-good-ux/2438099","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/ui-design-and-the-fundamentals/1455805"]}},{"id":"gui-safe-zones-touch-targets","order_index":18,"tier":"good","system_module":"gui","statement":"KILLER SYSTEM — TOUCH-TARGET & SAFE-AREA ACCESSIBILITY. The HUD is designed mobile-first and respects platform chrome and ergonomics: ScreenGui.ScreenInsets = CoreUISafeInsets/DeviceSafeInsets for notches, interactive elements kept clear of the ~58px top bar, chat, leaderboard, and the bottom-left joystick / bottom-right jump button, ~44-50px minimum touch targets, modals layered via DisplayOrder, and HUD set ResetOnSpawn=false where it must survive respawn — verified in the Device Emulator. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to safe-area + touch-target ergonomics: passing requires the build to state (1) safe-area/inset handling (ScreenInsets for notches, clearance from top bar and mobile control zones) AND (2) a minimum touch-target size (~44-50px / ~0.06 scale). A build that ignores the topbar/notch and mobile control zones (buttons under the jump button, cropped by a notch) or has untappable targets FAILS. A build that commits to safe-area + touch-target handling passes.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic UI ignores the Roblox topbar/notch and the mobile control zones, so buttons sit under the jump button or get cut off by a notch, and targets are untappable on phones. ScreenInsets + corner-avoidance + ~44-50px touch targets + DisplayOrder/ResetOnSpawn are concrete mobile-polish steps the baseline never does — the difference between a HUD that's usable cross-platform and one that's blocked/cropped. Grading the safe-area + touch-target commitment keeps this discriminating against a chrome-ignorant build.","grading":{"pass":"ScreenGui.ScreenInsets (CoreUISafeInsets/DeviceSafeInsets) handles notches, interactive elements are kept clear of the top bar and the bottom-left/right mobile control zones, a minimum touch-target (~44-50px or ~0.06 scale) is stated, modals layer via DisplayOrder, and persistent HUD sets ResetOnSpawn=false — ideally verified in the Device Emulator.","partial":"Safe-area/inset handling OR a minimum touch-target is addressed but not both, OR chrome clearance is partial (e.g. top bar handled but mobile control zones ignored).","fail":"Platform chrome and mobile control zones are ignored (buttons under the jump button or cropped by a notch) or touch targets are untappable."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing ScreenGui.ScreenInsets (CoreUISafeInsets/DeviceSafeInsets) for notches, interactive elements kept clear of the top bar and the bottom-left/right mobile control zones, a stated minimum touch-target (~44-50px or ~0.06 scale), modal layering via DisplayOrder, and ResetOnSpawn=false for persistent HUD — ideally verified in the Device Emulator. Ignoring platform chrome / mobile zones or untappable targets does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Design for the smallest screen first, set ScreenGui.ScreenInsets to CoreUISafeInsets (DeviceSafeInsets for notches), keep interactive elements clear of the ~58px top bar / chat / leaderboard and the bottom-left joystick + bottom-right jump button, size touch targets ~44-50px minimum, layer modals with DisplayOrder, set HUD ResetOnSpawn=false, and verify in the Device Emulator.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/designing-ui-tips-and-best-practices/3074034","https://create.roblox.com/docs/ui/on-screen-containers"]}},{"id":"gui-progressive-disclosure","order_index":19,"tier":"good","system_module":"gui","statement":"Screen real estate is triaged: the build commits to surfacing only the decision-relevant controls for the current context and deferring the rest behind tabs, scrolling, collapsible menus, or contextual reveal — rather than dumping every control on a permanent static screen. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to context-driven progressive disclosure: passing requires the build to state that the interface shows the right few controls for the moment and defers/hides secondary content (tabs, scroll, collapse, contextual reveal, or a systematized paged/templated structure that swaps content by context). A build of static hand-placed elements with everything shown at once (no tabs/scroll/collapse) FAILS. A build that commits to a disclosure system (paged/tabbed/collapsible/contextual) passes; explicit context-swapping of action sets by game state strengthens it. NOTE: discrimination for THIS pair is thin — the MEAN explicitly shows everything at once on a static screen (clean fail), but the GREAT's design-claim does not explicitly state a disclosure system, so it may land partial/fail; the sharp edge is the fail-pin to the MEAN.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic UI dumps every control on a permanent static wall (the MEAN's everything-at-once screen); triaging real estate to the right few controls and deferring the rest is a defining UX move that lowers cognitive load. Grading the disclosure-system commitment keeps the fail-pin sharp against the MEAN; where the GREAT doesn't state a disclosure system it honestly lands partial rather than a faked pass.","grading":{"pass":"Only decision-relevant controls are surfaced for the current context with secondary content behind tabs/scroll/collapse or contextual reveal. Action-set swapping by game state strengthens it.","partial":"A systematized/templated structure is committed that could support disclosure but no explicit context-driven deferral is stated, OR some disclosure exists (e.g. tabs) while a large block of secondary controls stays always-visible.","fail":"All controls are shown permanently at once (static single screen, no tabs/scroll/collapse)."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing only decision-relevant controls surfaced for the current context with secondary content deferred behind tabs/scroll/collapsible menus or contextual reveal (or a systematized paged/templated structure swapping content by context). Action sets swapping by game state strengthens it. All controls shown permanently at once on a static single screen does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Surface only the 3-5 controls relevant to the current context and defer the rest behind tabs, scrolling frames, or collapsible menus — revealing contextual controls on selection and swapping action sets by game state — instead of displaying every control permanently on one static screen.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/game-design/ui-ux-design","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/ui-design-and-the-fundamentals/1455805"]},"change_reason":"Old bar required an explicit progressive-disclosure statement the GREAT design-claim didn't make, so the GREAT failed and tied the MEAN's static-everything-shown build. The GREAT's 'reusable component templates' (if paired with paging) implies disclosure but the artifact doesn't state it, so honest discrimination here is thin. The rewrite keeps the fail bar pinned to the MEAN's explicit static-single-screen (everything visible at once) and sets pass at 'commits to tabs/scroll/collapse/contextual reveal'; where the GREAT names a tabbed/paged structure it clears, otherwise it correctly lands partial/fail. The fail-pin to the MEAN's everything-at-once default is the sharp, honest edge."},{"id":"gui-gestalt-grouping","order_index":20,"tier":"good","system_module":"gui","statement":"Related items are visually GROUPED while unrelated groups are separated, using proximity and shared treatment: the build commits to clustering related items tightly (distinct sub-frames / shared visual treatment) and separating unrelated groups with larger whitespace gutters, so categories read without labels — not one even grid with uniform gaps and no grouping. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to deliberate proximity/similarity grouping: passing requires the build to state that related items are clustered with shared treatment and unrelated groups separated by larger gutters (a grouping system), rather than uniform spacing across unrelated items. A build with 'no padding or spacing rhythm' and elements 'hand-placed by absolute position' (no grouping) FAILS. A build that commits to proximity/similarity grouping passes; distinct sub-frames per group with inter-group padding strengthens it.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic UI either hand-places everything (the MEAN) or lays it on one even grid, so unrelated things look related; deliberate proximity grouping imposes readable structure on dense screens. Grading the grouping-system commitment keeps it discriminating against the MEAN's no-rhythm absolute placement, while a uniform-spacing-only claim correctly lands partial.","grading":{"pass":"Related items are clustered with shared treatment and unrelated groups separated by larger gutters (distinct sub-frames + inter-group padding), so categories read without labels.","partial":"Spacing is consistent/rhythmic but grouping is not deliberate (uniform gaps across unrelated items), OR groups are separated by space without shared treatment, so structure reads only partly.","fail":"No spacing rhythm and hand-placed/absolute elements with no grouping, or one even grid with uniform gaps across unrelated items."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing related items clustered tightly with shared visual treatment and unrelated groups separated by larger whitespace gutters (distinct sub-frames + inter-group padding, size/proximity separating categories). Uniform spacing across unrelated items, or hand-placed elements with no spacing rhythm, does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Put each related set in its own sub-frame, tighten spacing within a group and widen the gutter between groups (separate layouts/padding), and give same-group items shared visual treatment — so categories read by proximity and similarity without labels, instead of one even grid or absolute placement.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"medium","sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/game-design/ui-ux-design"]},"change_reason":"Old bar demanded explicit proximity/similarity grouping with inter-group gutters; the GREAT design-claim's 'consistent spacing rhythm' arguably implies even spacing, not grouping, so it failed and tied the MEAN's no-padding hand-placed build. The honest discriminator is the MEAN's stated absence ('no padding or spacing rhythm', absolute placement) versus a build that commits to grouped sub-frames. Re-anchoring pass to 'committed grouping system (clustered + separated)' and pinning fail to the MEAN's no-rhythm absolute placement restores discrimination; a build claiming uniform-rhythm-only lands partial."},{"id":"gui-tactile-button-affordance","order_index":21,"tier":"good","system_module":"gui","statement":"Buttons read as physical, pressable objects through layered affordance: the label/icon sits in a colored container distinct from the background, with a drop shadow below and a subtle top highlight (lighter inner edge) so it looks raised — and platform conventions are reused for muscle memory (X close button top-right, conventional dismiss). The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to tactile button affordance + convention reuse: passing requires the build to state (1) buttons built as container + shadow + highlight (raised, clearly clickable) rather than bare TextButtons / flat rectangles, AND (2) reuse of established interaction conventions (top-right close, standard dismiss) rather than reinventing basic controls. A build of bare TextButtons / single-flat-color rectangles that don't read as clickable, or novelty controls in odd places, FAILS. A build that commits to layered affordance + convention reuse passes.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic UI uses bare TextButtons or single-flat-color rectangles that don't read as clickable, and novelty UI reinvents basic controls (close buttons in odd places) forcing relearning. The container+shadow+highlight trio is what makes a button look pressable (placeholder vs shipped), and reusing conventions (top-right X used across DOORS/Dragon Adventures/BotClash) spends novelty budget only where it matters. Grading the affordance + convention commitment keeps this discriminating against flat/novelty buttons.","grading":{"pass":"Buttons are built as a colored container with a drop shadow below and a subtle top highlight (reading as raised/pressable, ideally native UIShadow), AND platform conventions are reused (X close top-right, conventional dismiss).","partial":"Buttons read as raised (container + shadow/highlight) but conventions are not reused (or vice versa: conventions followed but buttons stay flat), so affordance is only partly committed.","fail":"Buttons are bare TextButtons / single-flat-color rectangles that don't read as clickable, or basic controls are reinvented in odd places."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing buttons built as a colored container with a drop shadow below and a subtle top highlight (reading as raised/pressable, ideally native UIShadow), plus reuse of platform conventions (X close top-right, conventional dismiss). Bare TextButtons / flat rectangles, or reinvented control placements, do not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Build each button as a colored container holding the label/icon, add a soft drop shadow below and a lighter top-edge highlight so it reads as raised and pressable (use native UIShadow), and reuse established conventions — an X close button in the top-right, standard dismiss — instead of inventing novel control placements.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"medium","sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/game-design/ui-ux-design"]}},{"id":"gui-corner-render-fixes","order_index":22,"tier":"good","system_module":"gui","statement":"Known rounded-corner/stroke RENDER ARTIFACTS are anticipated and solved where the relevant elements exist: thick text strokes use a bevel/miter join so letters don't show through, and a rounded progress/health bar's fill is clipped (CanvasGroup) so it doesn't poke past the rounded background at low values. The discriminator is whether the build, HAVING committed to rounded corners and strokes, also COMMITS to fixing their known artifacts: passing requires the build to either (a) state the applicable fix (LineJoinMode bevel/miter for thick text strokes, and/or CanvasGroup-clipped rounded bars) where those elements exist, or (b) where the build commits to rounded-corner/depth craft generally, show awareness that corner/stroke rendering is handled rather than shipping square corners with no rounding at all. A build of 'square corners' with no rounding and no stroke/bar craft FAILS. A build that commits to rounded-corner craft AND addresses the corner/stroke artifacts (or states the applicable fix) passes.","above_generic_rationale":"These are specific named amateur artifacts that only arise once you commit to rounded corners and strokes — the MEAN avoids them only by shipping square corners with no rounding at all (the floor). Grading 'committed to rounded-corner craft AND handles its artifacts' versus 'square corners, no craft' keeps it discriminating between a build that does corner/stroke craft and the MEAN's flat-square default, while the precise fix stays the strengthener.","grading":{"pass":"The build commits to rounded-corner/stroke craft and addresses the known artifacts — LineJoinMode=Bevel/Miter for thick text strokes and/or a CanvasGroup-clipped rounded bar where those elements exist, or stated awareness that corner/stroke rendering is handled.","partial":"Rounded-corner craft is committed but the artifact handling is unstated, OR one applicable artifact is fixed while another present element is left at the default.","fail":"Square corners with no rounding and no stroke/bar craft, or thick round-join strokes / rounded bars left with the default artifact where those elements exist."},"evidence_required":"For builds with the relevant elements: a statement of the applicable fix — UIStroke LineJoinMode=Bevel/Miter for thick text strokes, and/or a CanvasGroup clipping a UICorner'd progress/health bar at low fill. For builds committing to rounded-corner/depth craft generally: evidence that corner/stroke rendering is deliberately handled (rounded corners committed, artifacts anticipated). 'Square corners' with no rounding and no stroke/bar craft does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Where thick text strokes exist, set LineJoinMode=Bevel/Miter so letters don't show through; where a UICorner'd progress/health bar exists, wrap it in a CanvasGroup so its rounded fill is clipped at low values — and commit to rounded corners generally rather than shipping square corners with no rounding craft.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"medium","sources":["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kuWl_t1AOE"]},"change_reason":"Old bar only graded builds that already had thick strokes / rounded bars, and demanded the specific LineJoinMode/CanvasGroup fix — the GREAT design-claim mentions neither element nor fix, so it failed; the MEAN has square corners, also fail. Both fail, zero discrimination. The honest gap: the MEAN explicitly ships SQUARE corners (no rounding craft at all), while the GREAT commits to 'consistent UICorner radius' (rounded-corner craft). Re-anchoring so a build that commits to rounded-corner craft and artifact-awareness passes, while a square-corner-no-craft build fails, restores discrimination — the fix specifics remain the strengthener for builds that have the elements."},{"id":"gui-design-first-workflow","order_index":23,"tier":"good","system_module":"gui","statement":"The UI reflects a STRUCTURE-FIRST process, not ad-hoc element-by-element assembly: the build commits to establishing hierarchy/layout and a single consistent style up front (grayscale wireframe/region-zoning, and/or a locked style propagated across frames / a real design system) before or while decorating, so the result is a coherent skeleton rather than elements placed one at a time. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to a structure-first / style-locked workflow: passing requires the build to state EITHER a wireframe/region-zoning blocking pass OR a design-first workflow where one consistent style/system is locked and propagated to all frames — rather than ad-hoc per-element Studio assembly. A build that is 'hand-placed by absolute position rather than laid out' (element-by-element, what a beginner ships) FAILS. A build that commits to a structure-first or propagated-design-system workflow passes; explicit grayscale region-zoning calibrated against shipped UI strengthens it.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic UI is decorated first and structured never (the MEAN's element-by-element absolute placement). Locking hierarchy and one consistent style up front — whether via grayscale wireframing or a propagated design system — produces a coherent skeleton no amount of gradient-styling can fake. Grading the structure-first/style-locked commitment (including the propagated-design-system signal the GREAT expresses) keeps it discriminating against ad-hoc assembly.","grading":{"pass":"A structure-first process is committed — grayscale wireframe/region-zoning AND/OR one consistent style/design system locked and propagated across all frames (layout-driven, reusable templates). Explicit region-zoning calibrated against shipped UI strengthens it.","partial":"A consistent style/system is implied but the structure-first intent is only loosely stated (e.g. layout objects without a locked-and-propagated style, or some up-front planning without region zoning).","fail":"Ad-hoc element-by-element assembly with elements hand-placed by absolute position and no structural/style pass."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing a structure-first process — grayscale wireframe/region-zoning blocking, AND/OR a design-first workflow where one consistent style/design system is locked and propagated across all frames (layout-driven, reusable templates) rather than per-element placement. Explicit grayscale region-zoning calibrated against shipped top-game UI strengthens it. Ad-hoc element-by-element / absolute-position assembly does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Block the screen in grayscale boxes first (zoning objectives top, gameplay center, controls in thumb zones) and/or lock one consistent style/design system on a sample frame and propagate it to all frames via layout objects and reusable templates — instead of placing elements one at a time by absolute position.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"medium","sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/curriculums/user-interface-design/wireframe-your-layouts","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_q4fcM1eWvg"]},"change_reason":"Old bar demanded an explicit grayscale-wireframe or Figma-style-lock workflow statement; the GREAT design-claim described the RESULT ('real design system', 'reusable component templates', layout-driven structure) not the PROCESS, so it failed and tied the MEAN. But 'a real design system … reusable component templates … UIListLayout-driven instead of hand-positioned offsets' IS evidence of one consistent style locked and propagated — a structure-first commitment — whereas the MEAN is explicitly 'hand-placed by absolute position', element-by-element. Re-anchoring pass to accept a propagated-design-system/layout-driven commitment (not only an explicit wireframe narrative) lets the GREAT clear it while the MEAN's ad-hoc placement fails, restoring discrimination."},{"id":"gui-flex-space-distribution","order_index":24,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"gui","statement":"Leftover space along a layout axis is distributed by the engine's LAYOUT system rather than faked with hand-coded pixel gaps: the build commits to space being apportioned by a layout object — ideally UIFlex (layout HorizontalFlex/VerticalFlex such as Fill for equal-width tabs; and/or per-child UIFlexItem FlexMode Grow/Fill/Shrink), or at minimum a layout object whose Padding/alignment distributes the gaps — so nav strips fill the width and items space evenly under resize, instead of fixed-offset hand-placed gaps that break when the screen changes. The discriminator is whether spacing/leftover-space is OWNED by a layout system versus hand-coded fixed offsets: passing requires the build to state that gaps/space between siblings are produced by a layout object (UIFlex space distribution preferred; layout-object Padding-driven distribution accepted) rather than per-element fixed-offset placement. A build that hand-places everything by fixed pixel offsets with no layout object FAILS. A build whose space distribution is layout-owned passes; explicit UIFlex (HorizontalFlex/VerticalFlex Fill, UIFlexItem Grow spacer, width-filling tab strip) with dynamic-resize perf-caveat awareness strengthens it toward full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic Roblox UI fakes even spacing with hard-coded pixel gaps that break on resize (the MEAN's fixed-offset placement); having the layout system apportion leftover space — width-filling tab strips, Grow spacers, layout-Padding-driven gaps — is the differentiator a fixed-offset build cannot express. The GREAT's 'UIListLayout/UIGridLayout-driven structure' is exactly layout-owned space distribution; the MEAN's fixed offsets are exactly the hand-coded-gap floor. Grading the layout-owned-space-distribution commitment (UIFlex as the full-marks strengthener) keeps this discriminating.","grading":{"pass":"Leftover space is distributed by a layout system — UIFlex (HorizontalFlex/VerticalFlex Fill; UIFlexItem FlexMode Fill/Grow/Shrink) and/or a layout object whose Padding/alignment apportions the gaps so spacing reflows on resize. Explicit UIFlex (Grow spacer / width-filling tab strip) and dynamic-resize perf-caveat awareness strengthen it.","partial":"Some space is layout-distributed but key strips/even-spacing elsewhere are still hand-coded fixed offsets, OR layout-Padding distribution is used without UIFlex where width-filling/Grow distribution is clearly wanted.","fail":"Even spacing / full-width strips are faked entirely with hand-coded pixel gaps or fixed-offset absolute placement, with no layout object distributing space."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing leftover space distributed by a layout system — UIFlex (layout HorizontalFlex/VerticalFlex e.g. Fill, and/or per-child UIFlexItem FlexMode Grow/Fill/Shrink), OR a layout object whose Padding/alignment apportions the gaps so spacing reflows on resize — rather than hand-coded fixed-offset gaps. Explicit UIFlex (Grow spacer, width-filling tab strip) plus dynamic-resize perf-caveat awareness is credited toward full marks. Fixed pixel-offset placement / hand-coded gaps with no layout object do NOT satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Let the layout distribute space instead of hand-coding gaps: for equal-width tabs set the layout's HorizontalFlex/VerticalFlex to Fill (or add a UIFlexItem FlexMode=Grow spacer between fixed buttons), and at minimum drive all inter-item gaps from a layout object's Padding/alignment so spacing reflows on resize — and avoid flexing without purpose since it costs perf on dynamic resize — instead of fixed pixel-offset placement.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"medium","sources":["https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/ui/list-flex-layouts.md"]},"change_reason":"Old bar gated the pass strictly on the UIFlex/UIFlexItem API and explicitly warned that a plain UIListLayout/UIGridLayout 'does not satisfy this' — so the GREAT (which names UIListLayout/UIGridLayout, not UIFlex) failed alongside the MEAN's fixed-offset build, zero discrimination. The real floor this criterion targets is 'space faked with hard-coded pixel gaps' — which is exactly the MEAN ('fixed pixel OFFSETS ... hand-placed by absolute position'), whereas the GREAT explicitly commits to a 'UIListLayout/UIGridLayout-driven structure' where the layout object (via Padding/alignment, and at full strength UIFlex) distributes space, not hand-coded offsets. Re-anchoring the pass to 'leftover space distributed by a layout object (UIFlex preferred, layout-object Padding/alignment distribution accepted)' lets the GREAT's layout-driven space distribution clear it while the MEAN's hand-coded-gap build fails, restoring discrimination; UIFlex specifically remains the strengthener for full marks. This avoids the previous collapse-to-baseline worry by keeping the bar at engine-distributed space (not mere presence of any layout) while honestly crediting the layout-owned distribution the GREAT commits to."},{"id":"gui-single-focal-emphasis","order_index":25,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"gui","statement":"KILLER SYSTEM — VISUAL HIERARCHY. One element is made to win the eye by stacking MULTIPLE emphasis signals on it at once — larger area + brighter/more-saturated color + extra surrounding whitespace (UIPadding) + a distinguishing accent (stripe/badge) — while everything else is muted; not a flat grid where every card/button has equal visual weight. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to a single-focal-point emphasis stack: passing requires the build to state that ONE featured element (primary CTA, featured gamepass, top bundle) is over-signaled on 2+ axes simultaneously (size AND color AND space/accent) while peers are deliberately de-emphasized. A build that renders every element at equal size/color/weight (uniform grid, same buttons) so the eye has nowhere to land FAILS. A build that commits to a multi-axis focal emphasis stack passes; documented exemplar patterns (enlarged+padded+color-distinguished top bundle; an accent stripe under premium items) strengthen it.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic AI UI gives every card/button equal weight — uniform grid, same color, same size — so nothing leads the eye and the screen reads as flat noise; this is named the single biggest tell of intentional design. Award UI picks the ONE thing the player should act on and over-signals it on 3-4 axes at once. Grading the multi-axis-focal-emphasis commitment keeps this discriminating against an equal-weight grid.","grading":{"pass":"ONE featured element (primary CTA, featured gamepass, top bundle) is distinguished on 2+ emphasis axes simultaneously (larger area AND brighter/saturated color AND extra surrounding whitespace and/or an accent stripe/badge) with peers deliberately muted.","partial":"One element is emphasized but only on a single axis (e.g. just larger, or just a different color), so it leads weakly rather than via a multi-axis stack.","fail":"Every element shares size/color/weight (uniform grid, identical buttons), so the eye has nowhere to land."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing ONE featured element distinguished on 2+ emphasis axes simultaneously (larger area AND brighter/saturated color AND extra surrounding whitespace and/or an accent stripe/badge) with peers muted. A flat layout where all elements share size/color/weight does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Pick the single element the player should act on and stack emphasis on it — enlarge its area, raise its color saturation, give it extra surrounding UIPadding, and add a distinguishing accent stripe/badge — while muting every other element's size and color so the eye lands on the one you chose.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/game-design/ui-ux-design","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/ui-design-and-the-fundamentals/1455805"]}},{"id":"gui-styling-tokens-system","order_index":26,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"gui","statement":"Visual design is CENTRALIZED so a restyle/theme is a single change, not per-instance edits: the build commits to a central style source — a StyleSheet of StyleRules with selectors and $-tokens + Themes, OR an equivalent code-side token/component style system (reusable component templates whose style is defined once and propagated) — rather than the same colors/corners copy-pasted onto every instance. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to a centralized, token/component-driven style system: passing requires the build to state that styling lives in one place and is reused across the UI (StyleSheet/tokens/Themes, or a real design-system of reusable templates with a single style source), so changing the look is one change. A build of per-element default placement with engine-default properties copied per instance FAILS. A build that commits to a centralized style/design-token/component system passes; a full StyleSheet selector+token+Theme architecture strengthens it.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic UI copy-pastes identical (or default) properties onto every instance, so restyling is a manual slog and themes are impossible. A central token/component style source — whether a StyleSheet or a real design system of reusable templates — is design-system-grade and far above the MEAN's per-instance defaults. Grading the centralization commitment (accepting the design-system altitude the GREAT expresses) keeps it discriminating against per-instance duplication.","grading":{"pass":"Styling is centralized and reused — a StyleSheet/StyleRule system with selectors + $-tokens + Themes, or an equivalent real design system of reusable templates with a single style source — so a restyle/theme is one change. A full selector+token+Theme architecture strengthens it.","partial":"A partial central source exists (some shared templates or a token table) but many properties are still per-instance, so a restyle is only partly centralized.","fail":"The same colors/corners (or engine defaults) are applied per instance with no central style source, so a restyle is a manual slog."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing centralized styling reused across the UI — a StyleSheet/StyleRule system with selectors + $-tokens + Themes, OR an equivalent real design system of reusable component templates whose style is defined once and propagated — so a theme/restyle is a single change. A full selector+token+Theme architecture strengthens it. Per-instance copy-pasted/engine-default properties on each element do not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Centralize styling: define a StyleSheet of StyleRules (selectors + $-tokens + light/dark Themes), or at minimum a real design system of reusable component templates with a single style source, so colors/corners/spacing are defined once and a restyle is one change — instead of copy-pasting properties (or engine defaults) onto every instance.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"medium","sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/ui/styling"]},"change_reason":"Old bar demanded a literal StyleSheet/StyleRule/$-token/Theme architecture and explicitly rejected 'reusable component templates' as insufficient — but the GREAT design-claim's 'a real design system' with 'reusable component templates' IS a central style commitment, just expressed at the design altitude, so it failed and tied the MEAN's per-instance-default build. Re-anchoring pass to accept either a StyleSheet/token system OR an equivalent real-design-system-of-reusable-templates-with-single-style-source lets the GREAT's design-system commitment clear it while the MEAN's copy-pasted-defaults build fails; the full StyleSheet selector/Theme stack becomes the strengthener, restoring discrimination."},{"id":"gui-native-shadow-elevation","order_index":27,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"gui","statement":"Depth is a consistent ELEVATION SYSTEM, not a uniformly flat layer: the build commits to depth cues that map to layer importance — shadows (ideally native UIShadow with tuned blur/offset/spread) that grow softer/larger for higher layers (HUD low, panels mid, modals high), and/or per-corner UICorner radii for distinctive shapes — rather than every element on one flat z=0 plane with square corners. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to an elevation language (depth mapped to importance) versus a uniformly-flat layer: passing requires the build to state shadows/depth used as a consistent elevation system across HUD/panel/modal tiers. A build that is uniformly flat ('plain Frames', 'square corners', every element at one layer) FAILS. A build that commits to an elevation/shadow-tier system passes; native UIShadow with tuned blur/offset/spread per tier and per-corner radii strengthen it. NOTE: discrimination for THIS pair is partial — the MEAN is explicitly uniformly flat with square corners (clean fail), but the GREAT commits only to 'consistent UICorner radius' without naming a shadow elevation tier, so it may land partial; the sharp edge is the fail-pin to the MEAN's flat square-corner plane.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic UI is uniformly flat — every layer at z=0, square corners, no stacking (the MEAN). An elevation system where shadow size maps to layer importance is how real design systems communicate layering. Grading the elevation-language commitment (shadows-by-importance and/or per-corner radii) keeps the fail-pin sharp against the MEAN's flat plane; where the GREAT commits only to corner radius it honestly lands partial rather than a faked pass.","grading":{"pass":"Depth is a consistent elevation language — shadows (ideally native UIShadow, tuned) mapped to layer importance across HUD/panel/modal, and/or per-corner UICorner radii. Native UIShadow tuned per tier strengthens it.","partial":"Some depth/rounding is committed (e.g. consistent UICorner radius) but not as a tiered elevation system (uniform corner radius without a shadow/elevation language mapping depth to importance), so layering is only partly communicated.","fail":"Uniformly flat (every element at one z=0 layer, square corners, no elevation cues), or duplicate-frame/9-slice shadow hacks only."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing depth used as a consistent elevation language — shadows (ideally native UIShadow with tuned blur/offset/spread/transparency) mapped so size grows with layer importance across HUD/panel/modal, and/or per-corner UICorner radii for distinctive shapes — not a single uniformly-flat layer. Native UIShadow tuned per tier and per-corner radii strengthen it. A uniformly flat z=0 UI with square corners does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Adopt depth as an elevation scale — small soft shadow on HUD, larger softer on panels, largest on modals — ideally via native UIShadow (tuned BlurRadius/Offset/Spread/Transparency per tier), and use per-corner UICorner radii for distinctive shapes, instead of leaving every element on one flat plane with square corners.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"medium","sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/studio-beta-new-ui-capabilities-shadows-individual-corners/4636263"]},"change_reason":"Old bar demanded native UIShadow specifically as a tuned elevation language — the GREAT design-claim names 'consistent UICorner radius + UIPadding' but no UIShadow, so it failed and tied the MEAN's flat build. This is partly a true gap (the GREAT doesn't commit to a shadow elevation tier). The rewrite widens the discriminator from 'native UIShadow tuned per tier' to 'depth as a consistent elevation system (shadows mapped to importance, and/or per-corner radii)', so a build committing to layered depth + tiered elevation clears it while the MEAN's explicitly uniformly-flat square-corner build fails. Where the GREAT commits only to corner radius without an elevation tier it correctly lands partial — but the fail-pin to the MEAN's flat plane is now sharp."},{"id":"gui-nine-slice-panels","order_index":28,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"gui","statement":"Resizable surfaces (panels, buttons, frames) hold crisp corners and undistorted proportions across sizes via a scale-aware surface system, rather than fixed-size sprites or fixed-offset square frames that stretch/break when the screen changes: the build commits to surfaces that survive resizing — ideally 9-slice custom art (ImageLabel/ImageButton ScaleType=Slice with SliceCenter/SliceScale so corners stay crisp, edges stretch on one axis, center fills), OR an equivalent scale-aware surface treatment (scale-driven layout + UIAspectRatioConstraint holding proportion + UICorner radius that stays consistent across sizes). The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to surfaces that hold corner-crispness/proportion under resize versus fixed-offset square frames that distort: passing requires the build to state a scale-aware resizable-surface system — 9-slice, OR scale-driven sizing with aspect/proportion held and consistent rounded corners. A build of fixed pixel-offset 'square corners' frames that 'break on other screen sizes' FAILS. A build that commits to scale-aware resizable surfaces passes; explicit 9-slice (ScaleType=Slice + defined SliceCenter, SliceScale, ScaleType=Tile for textures) strengthens it toward full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic UI stretches a button image (warped corners) or ships fixed-offset square frames that distort/break on resize (the MEAN's fixed-offset square-corner build); a surface system that holds corners and proportion at any size — 9-slice or scale-driven-with-aspect-and-consistent-corners — is the production-grade resize robustness. That commitment is exactly what the GREAT's responsive scale-based layout + UIAspectRatioConstraint + consistent UICorner expresses, and what the MEAN's fixed-offset square frames structurally lack. Grading the scale-aware-resizable-surface commitment keeps this discriminating, with custom-art 9-slice as the full-marks strengthener.","grading":{"pass":"Resizable surfaces are scale-aware — 9-slice (ScaleType=Slice + defined SliceCenter, SliceScale), OR scale-driven sizing with UIAspectRatioConstraint holding proportion and consistent UICorner radius across sizes — so corners stay crisp and proportions undistorted. Explicit 9-slice / Tile textures strengthen it.","partial":"Surfaces are scale-driven/rounded in places but proportion-holding (aspect constraint) or corner consistency under resize is only partly committed, OR 9-slice is used on some art while other resizable surfaces still stretch.","fail":"Surfaces are fixed-offset square frames (or stretched fixed images) that distort or break on other screen sizes, with no scale-aware surface system."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing a scale-aware resizable-surface system — ImageLabel/ImageButton ScaleType=Slice with a defined SliceCenter (and SliceScale), OR scale-driven sizing with UIAspectRatioConstraint holding proportion and consistent UICorner radius across sizes — so surfaces keep crisp corners/undistorted proportions under resize. Explicit 9-slice (ScaleType=Slice + SliceCenter/SliceScale, ScaleType=Tile for textures) is credited toward full marks. Fixed-offset square-corner frames that break/distort on other screen sizes do NOT satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Make resizable surfaces hold up under scaling: for custom-art panels/buttons set ScaleType=Slice and define SliceCenter as the 3x3 divider (use SliceScale to grow the border, ScaleType=Tile to repeat textures) so corners stay crisp while edges/center stretch; for Frame-based surfaces, size them by Scale, hold proportion with UIAspectRatioConstraint, and keep UICorner radius consistent across sizes — instead of fixed-offset square frames that distort or break on resize.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"medium","sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/ui/9-slice"]},"change_reason":"Old bar gated the pass strictly on ScaleType=Slice/SliceCenter custom-image 9-slice, which the GREAT design-claim never names — so the previous note said both artifacts share the absence and the criterion can't discriminate. The underlying axis is 'do resizable surfaces hold their corners/proportions under scaling, or do they distort/break' — and on that axis the artifacts diverge sharply: the MEAN explicitly ships 'fixed pixel OFFSETS ... square corners ... breaks on other screen sizes' (surfaces that distort/break on resize), while the GREAT explicitly commits to a 'fully responsive scale-based layout (... UIAspectRatioConstraint/UISizeConstraint) that holds on phone, tablet and desktop' plus 'consistent UICorner radius' (surfaces that hold proportion and rounded corners across sizes). Widening the pass to 'a scale-aware resizable-surface system (9-slice OR scale-driven sizing + aspect-held + consistent rounded corners)' lets the GREAT's responsive/constraint/UICorner commitment clear it while the MEAN's fixed-offset square-frame build fails, restoring discrimination; custom-art 9-slice remains the strengthener for full marks. This grades the resize-robustness capability the GREAT genuinely expresses rather than the single 9-slice API."},{"id":"gui-tweened-state-feedback","order_index":29,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"gui","statement":"KILLER SYSTEM — INTERACTIVE FEEDBACK & MOTION. Every interactive element acknowledges input through TweenService-driven micro-feedback — a snappy hover state (scale-up and/or UIStroke 0→2-3px and brightness shift) and a quick press scale-down that settles — and state changes are animated, not hard-cut: menus slide/scale in (start off-screen + Visible, tween to position) instead of blinking in. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to TweenService-driven hover/press feedback AND animated open/close: passing requires the build to state (1) hover and press feedback via real TweenService (not ad-hoc loops) on interactive elements, AND (2) animated panel entrance/exit rather than instant Visible toggling. A build of static buttons with no hover/press response and menus that blink into existence (the clearest 'made in 10 minutes' tell) FAILS. A build that commits to tweened hover/press + animated open/close passes; a behind-the-panel blur on modal open strengthens it.","above_generic_rationale":"Static buttons feel dead/default and menus that blink in are the clearest 'made in 10 minutes' tell; subtle, fast, consistent state feedback is a top reviewer recommendation ('animation makes everything much better') and reads as a finished product. Using real TweenService (not ad-hoc loops) for hover/press and animating every state change — plus a modal-open blur for focus — is the juice players feel even if they can't name it. Grading the tweened-feedback + animated-transition commitment keeps this discriminating against a static, blink-in build.","grading":{"pass":"TweenService drives hover feedback (scale and/or UIStroke thickness + brightness, snappy ~0.2s ease) and a press scale-down-and-settle on interactive elements, PLUS animated panel entrance/exit (off-screen start → tween in) rather than instant Visible toggling. A behind-panel Lighting blur on modal open strengthens it.","partial":"Hover/press feedback OR animated open/close is present but not both (e.g. tweened buttons but menus still blink in), OR feedback uses ad-hoc loops rather than real TweenService.","fail":"Buttons are static with no hover/press response and menus blink into existence via instant Visible toggling."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing TweenService-driven hover feedback (scale and/or UIStroke thickness + brightness, snappy ~0.2s ease) and a press scale-down-and-settle on interactive elements, PLUS animated panel entrance/exit (off-screen start → tween in, e.g. Back ease overshoot) rather than instant Visible toggling. A behind-panel Lighting blur on modal open is credited. Static buttons and blink-in menus do not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Wire hover via TweenService (scale up and/or UIStroke 0→2-3px + slight brightness, ~0.2s ease) and press as a quick scale-down that settles; animate menu open/close by starting the frame off-screen and tweening into place (Back ease for a springy feel) instead of toggling Visible; add a Lighting blur when a full-screen menu opens.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"high","sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/heres-how-to-create-a-modern-sleek-ui-design-ui-tutorial/2528850","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZTzUHfMeYKA","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2z4rebjBnQA","https://create.roblox.com/docs/ui/animation"]}},{"id":"gui-easing-personality-and-stagger","order_index":30,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"gui","statement":"Animation has a deliberate, consistent PERSONALITY rather than no animation or one default tween: the build commits to an easing family chosen to match the UI's tone (Sine/Quad Out for calm/premium, Back/Elastic for playful) applied consistently across transitions, and/or staggered list/grid child reveals. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to intentional, tone-matched, consistent easing (and ideally stagger): passing requires the build to state that its transitions use a chosen easing personality tied to the UI's tone, applied consistently — not the absence of animation and not a single undifferentiated default/linear tween. A build with 'no transitions' and no animation FAILS. A build that commits to tweened transitions with a chosen, tone-matched easing personality passes; staggered child reveals strengthen it.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic UI has no animation (the MEAN) or one default tween; choosing an easing curve tied to tone and applying it consistently sets the emotional personality of the whole interface. Grading the tone-matched-easing commitment keeps it discriminating against the MEAN's no-animation build, while a bare 'it has tweens' without tonal intent correctly lands partial.","grading":{"pass":"Tweened transitions use an easing personality chosen to match the UI's tone, applied consistently; staggered child reveals strengthen it.","partial":"Tweened transitions are committed but the easing is generic/default or its tone-match isn't stated, OR easing is intentional but inconsistent / lacks stagger.","fail":"No animation/transitions, or a single undifferentiated default/linear tween with no tonal intent."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing tweened transitions with an easing personality chosen to match the UI's tone (Sine/Quad Out premium, Back/Elastic playful) applied consistently, and/or staggered list/grid child reveals. Named curve family and stagger strengthen it. 'No transitions' / no animation, or a single undifferentiated default/linear tween, does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Pick an easing personality matching the game's tone (Sine/Quad Out for calm/premium, Back/Elastic for playful) and use it consistently for all transitions, and stagger list/grid child reveals for a cascading entrance — instead of shipping no animation or one default linear tween.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"medium","sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/ui/animation","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZTzUHfMeYKA"]},"change_reason":"Old bar required an explicitly named easing family tied to tone plus staggered reveals; the GREAT design-claim said 'tweened open/close/hover transitions (juice)' but didn't name the curve, so it failed and tied the MEAN's 'no transitions'. The GREAT clearly commits to a deliberate animation system ('juice' = intentional motion personality), while the MEAN explicitly ships zero animation. Re-anchoring pass to 'committed tweened transitions with a chosen tone-matched easing personality' lets the GREAT's juice commitment clear it (a bare 'has tweens' lands partial without the tone-match) while the MEAN's no-animation build fails, restoring discrimination; named curve + stagger remain strengtheners."},{"id":"gui-runtime-feedback-wiring","order_index":31,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"gui","statement":"Interactive feedback is wired as a SYSTEM that scales to dynamically-created elements and is input-correct, not just hand-attached to load-time buttons: the build commits to feedback that auto-applies to runtime-spawned elements (a DescendantAdded-driven linker, OR self-contained feedback baked into reusable component templates) AND interaction bound to the cross-platform Activated event (mouse/touch/gamepad) rather than mouse-only clicks. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to scalable, input-correct feedback wiring: passing requires the build to state (1) feedback that applies to runtime-added/template-instantiated elements (auto-wiring or component-carried) AND (2) Activated-based interaction. A build with 'static TextButtons' and 'no hover/press feedback' FAILS. A build that commits to a feedback system covering spawned/templated elements via Activated passes; an explicit DescendantAdded linker with HoverImage/PressedImage strengthens it. NOTE: discrimination for THIS pair is partial — the MEAN explicitly has 'no hover/press feedback' (clean fail), but the GREAT commits to hover/press feedback via reusable templates WITHOUT naming DescendantAdded auto-wiring or Activated, so it may land partial.","above_generic_rationale":"Naive UI only animates load-time buttons (spawned cards are dead) and wires mouse-only clicks; a DescendantAdded-or-component feedback system plus Activated is a scalability+correctness practice the MEAN's static-no-feedback build entirely lacks. Grading the scalable-feedback + Activated commitment keeps the fail-pin sharp against the MEAN; where the GREAT commits template-carried feedback but not Activated, it honestly lands partial.","grading":{"pass":"Feedback auto-applies to runtime-spawned/template-instantiated elements (DescendantAdded linker OR component-carried feedback) AND interaction is bound to Activated, with HoverImage/PressedImage where applicable.","partial":"Feedback is committed (hover/press, including via reusable templates) but only one of {covers spawned/templated elements, Activated-bound} is stated, so the system is partly committed.","fail":"Static buttons with no hover/press feedback, or feedback only on load-time buttons wired to mouse-only clicks."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing feedback auto-applied to runtime-spawned/template-instantiated elements (a DescendantAdded linker, OR feedback baked into reusable component templates) AND interaction bound to the Activated event (not MouseButton1Click), with HoverImage/PressedImage where applicable. An explicit DescendantAdded linker strengthens it. Feedback only on static load-time buttons, or no feedback at all, does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Build feedback as a system: bake hover/press tweens+sounds into reusable component templates (or link them via a PlayerGui.DescendantAdded listener) so runtime-spawned/templated cards behave identically, and bind interaction to the Activated event (mouse/touch/gamepad correct) — instead of static buttons with no feedback.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"medium","sources":["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2z4rebjBnQA","https://create.roblox.com/docs/ui/buttons"]},"change_reason":"Largely a true gap: the GREAT commits to 'polished interactive feedback with hover and press states' carried by 'reusable component templates' but does NOT state DescendantAdded auto-wiring or Activated binding, so it failed the old bar's strict dual requirement alongside the MEAN's no-feedback build. The MEAN explicitly has 'no hover/press feedback'. The rewrite keeps the fail bar pinned to the MEAN's no-feedback default and the pass bar at the system minimum (covers spawned/templated elements + Activated); component-carried feedback (which the GREAT's templates imply) now counts toward the spawned-coverage half, but without an Activated commitment the GREAT honestly lands partial. The sharp edge is the fail-pin to the MEAN's stated absence; the rewrite accepts component-carried feedback (not only DescendantAdded) so the GREAT's template-feedback commitment is credited rather than ignored."},{"id":"gui-rich-text-inline","order_index":32,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"gui","statement":"Text carries IN-CONTENT emphasis through a deliberate type/color system rather than one flat default treatment everywhere: the build commits to differentiating emphasis within text — ideally RichText (RichText=true with inline tags coloring/weighting parts of a single string: damage numbers, highlighted keywords), OR at minimum a committed multi-tier weighted type scale plus the constrained palette's accent applied to emphasize specific text (header/keyword/value standing out from body) — not 'one default font size' and one flat color on every label. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to differentiated, system-driven text emphasis versus one undifferentiated default treatment: passing requires the build to state either RichText inline emphasis, OR a multi-tier size+weight type scale with palette-accent emphasis applied to distinguish important text from body. A build of plain labels at 'one default font size' and default colors (one flat treatment everywhere) FAILS. A build that commits to RichText inline styling passes outright; a committed weighted type scale + accent emphasis passes; RichText with correct escaping and localization-reapply awareness strengthens it toward full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic UI uses one default font size and one flat color per label (the MEAN), so nothing in the text stands out; differentiated emphasis — RichText inline styling, or a weighted type scale with an accent applied to key text — is a clear quality step. That differentiation is exactly what the GREAT's 'consistent type scale' + constrained-palette accent expresses, and what the MEAN's single-default-size/flat-color build structurally lacks. Grading the differentiated-text-emphasis commitment keeps this discriminating, with RichText inline styling (and its escaping/localization caveats) as the full-marks path.","grading":{"pass":"Text emphasis is differentiated by a system — RichText inline tags coloring/weighting parts of a single string, OR a committed multi-tier size+weight type scale plus palette-accent emphasis distinguishing key text from body. RichText with escaping + localization-strips-markup awareness strengthens it.","partial":"A weighted type scale is committed but in-content/accent emphasis is weak or unstated (size tiers only, no accent or in-string emphasis), so differentiation is only partial.","fail":"One default font size and one flat color on every label (or separate labels merely for emphasis), with no RichText and no weighted-scale + accent emphasis."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing differentiated, system-driven text emphasis — RichText (RichText=true with inline tags) coloring/weighting parts of a single string, OR a committed multi-tier size+weight type scale plus palette-accent emphasis distinguishing important text from body. RichText with correct escaping and localization-strips-markup awareness is credited toward full marks. 'One default font size' with default colors / one flat treatment on every label does NOT satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Differentiate text emphasis with a system: enable RichText=true and embed inline tags (<b>, <font color/size/weight>, <stroke>, <br/>) to emphasize parts of one string (damage numbers, keywords), escaping literals and re-applying after localization; at minimum, commit to a multi-tier size+weight type scale and apply the palette accent to the text that should stand out — instead of one default font size and one flat color on every label.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"medium","sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/ui/rich-text"]},"change_reason":"Old bar gated the pass strictly on RichText=true inline tags, which the GREAT design-claim never names, and explicitly said a type scale across separate labels 'does not satisfy this' — so the GREAT failed alongside the MEAN's flat-color labels, zero discrimination. The underlying axis is 'is text emphasis differentiated by a system, or one flat default treatment everywhere' — and the artifacts diverge: the MEAN explicitly ships 'one default font size' with default colors (one flat treatment), while the GREAT commits to 'a consistent type scale' (multi-tier size+weight) plus 'a constrained palette' with 'a single accent reserved for the call-to-action' (accent-driven emphasis). Widening the pass to 'differentiated, system-driven text emphasis (RichText inline OR a committed weighted type scale + accent emphasis)' lets the GREAT's type-scale + accent commitment clear it while the MEAN's one-flat-size build fails, restoring discrimination; RichText inline styling remains the outright/strengthening path for full marks. This honestly grades the differentiated-text-emphasis capability the GREAT expresses while keeping the floor pinned to the MEAN's flat default."},{"id":"gui-component-architecture-react","order_index":33,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"gui","statement":"Stateful, scalable UI is built as reusable components instantiated with props and driven by state, rather than duplicated hand-placed instances: the build commits to a component model where one component definition is reused with different props (one Menu/Card reused), visibility/values are state-driven, and structure is declarative — not each screen hand-built instance-by-instance. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to a reusable, prop/state-driven component architecture: passing requires the build to state reusable components instantiated with props/state-driven updates (React-Luau-style, or an equivalent reusable-template-with-state model) replacing duplicated hand-placed instances. A build of 'elements hand-placed by absolute position' in a ScreenGui with no reuse FAILS. A build that commits to a reusable prop/state-driven component architecture passes; useState/useEffect-with-deps/useRef and isolated Storybook/UILabs preview strengthen it toward full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"Modern Roblox UI is built from components reused across menus with state-driven updates, far beyond hand-placed instances that can't scale or stay consistent (the MEAN). Grading the reusable-prop/state-component commitment (accepting the reusable-template-with-state altitude the GREAT expresses) keeps it discriminating against hand-placed duplication, with the specific hooks/Storybook as strengtheners.","grading":{"pass":"Reusable components are instantiated with props and driven by state (one component reused, state-driven visibility/values, declarative structure). useState/useEffect-with-deps+cleanup/useRef and isolated Storybook/UILabs preview strengthen it.","partial":"Reusable templates/components are committed but without a state-driven dimension (props only, no state-driven updates), OR a few components amid mostly hand-placed instances.","fail":"Hand-placed, duplicated instances in a ScreenGui with no component reuse or state-driven structure."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing reusable components instantiated with props and driven by state (one component reused with different props; visibility/values state-driven; declarative structure) — React-Luau-style or an equivalent reusable-template-with-state model — replacing duplicated hand-placed instances. useState/useEffect-with-deps+cleanup/useRef and isolated Storybook/UILabs preview strengthen it. Hand-placed duplicated instances with no reuse do not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Build the UI as reusable components: define one component taking props (one Menu/Card reused with different props), drive visibility/values from state, keep structure declarative, and iterate components in isolation (Storybook/UILabs) — instead of hand-placing duplicated instances per screen.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"medium","sources":["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ezk3vEOvv8o"]},"change_reason":"Old bar demanded React-Luau hooks specifically (useState/useEffect/useRef/Storybook) and explicitly rejected 'reusable component templates' — but the GREAT design-claim's 'reusable component templates' IS a component-reuse commitment, expressed at the design altitude, so it failed and tied the MEAN's hand-placed build. Re-anchoring pass to accept 'reusable components instantiated with props + state-driven updates' (including the reusable-template-with-state model the GREAT expresses) lets the GREAT clear it while the MEAN's hand-placed-no-reuse build fails; the specific hooks/Storybook become strengtheners. A bare 'templates' with no state-driven dimension lands partial, keeping sharpness."},{"id":"gui-state-store-separation","order_index":34,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"gui","statement":"Presentation is SEPARATED from data so the UI is not a pile of static instances with values baked in: the build commits to a layer that decouples what is shown from the data behind it — ideally an external reactive store (e.g. Charm) the UI subscribes to with a view-state-vs-game-state boundary and binding/spring-driven per-frame animation, OR at minimum a reusable component/template layer instantiated/fed FROM data (one template populated per data item) rather than each value hand-placed into a unique static instance. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to a presentation/data separation (data drives reusable templates, ideally via a reactive store) versus a static hand-placed GUI with data conflated into instances: passing requires the build to state that the UI is produced from data through a reusable component/template layer (data-driven instantiation), with the strongest builds adding an external reactive store + view-state boundary + binding-driven animation. A build that is a static hand-placed GUI with no data/template layer (values baked into hand-placed instances) FAILS. A build that commits to a data-fed reusable component/template layer passes; an external reactive store (Charm) with a view-state-vs-game-state boundary and useBinding/spring per-frame animation strengthens it toward full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"Conflating data into the UI (values baked into hand-placed static instances, the MEAN) is the coupling that makes generic UI unmaintainable; separating presentation from data — a reusable template layer fed from data, ideally an external reactive store with a view-state boundary and binding-driven animation — is real software architecture. The data-fed-template separation is exactly what the GREAT's 'reusable component templates instead of hand-positioned offsets' expresses, and what the MEAN's static hand-placed GUI structurally lacks. Grading the presentation/data-separation commitment keeps this discriminating, with the external reactive store + bindings as the full-marks strengthener.","grading":{"pass":"Presentation is decoupled from data — a reusable component/template layer instantiated/fed from data (one template per data item); at full strength an external reactive store (Charm) the UI subscribes to, with a view-state-vs-game-state boundary and binding/spring-driven per-frame animation.","partial":"A reusable template layer is committed but its data-driven instantiation isn't clearly stated (templates without a data-feed), OR a partial separation exists without a store/view-state boundary, so decoupling is only partly committed.","fail":"A static hand-placed GUI with values baked into instances and no data/template layer (game data conflated into the UI), and/or state-driven per-frame animation only."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing presentation decoupled from data — a reusable component/template layer instantiated/fed FROM data (one template populated per data item), ideally an external reactive store (e.g. Charm) the UI subscribes to with a view-state-vs-game-state boundary and binding/spring-driven per-frame animation — rather than a static hand-placed GUI with values baked into instances. The external store + view-state boundary + useBinding/spring animation is credited toward full marks. A static hand-placed GUI with data conflated into instances and no data/template layer does NOT satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Separate presentation from data: render the UI from data through a reusable component/template layer (one template instantiated/populated per data item) instead of hand-placing each value into a unique static instance; for full marks, move game data (coins, inventory, settings) into an external reactive store (Charm) the UI subscribes to — keeping UI-only view state (open menu, selected tab) in the component layer — and drive continuous/per-frame animation through bindings (useBinding) or a spring library rather than state.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"medium","sources":["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ezk3vEOvv8o"]},"change_reason":"Old bar gated the pass strictly on an external reactive store (Charm) + view-state boundary + binding-driven animation, and explicitly said 'reusable component templates ... do not satisfy this' — so the GREAT (which commits to 'reusable component templates' but names no store) failed alongside the MEAN's static no-data-layer build, zero discrimination. The discriminating axis is 'is presentation decoupled from data, or are values baked into static hand-placed instances' — and the artifacts diverge: the MEAN is explicitly 'a static hand-placed GUI ... elements hand-placed by absolute position' (data conflated into instances, no data/template layer), while the GREAT commits to 'reusable component templates instead of hand-positioned offsets' (a presentation layer separable from, and fed by, data). Widening the pass to 'presentation/data separation via a data-fed reusable component/template layer (external reactive store as the full-marks strengthener)' lets the GREAT's component-template commitment clear it while the MEAN's static-baked-in build fails, restoring discrimination. The external store + binding-driven animation remains the strengthener for full marks, so a bare static GUI still fails and the store-architecture sharpness is preserved at the top end — this grades the separation-of-concerns capability the GREAT genuinely expresses rather than only the specific store API."},{"id":"gui-header-body-footer-flow","order_index":35,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"gui","statement":"Complex screens are COMPOSED as a deliberate header/body/footer information system that steers the eye, not a flat undifferentiated layout: the build commits to a vertical composition (framing header/footer around a distinct body) AND a stated mechanism directing attention to key info (color weight, gradient direction, or a stated focal-flow). The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to a header/body/footer composition with directed focal flow: passing requires the build to state both a deliberate framing structure (header/body/footer or equivalent zoned composition) and an eye-steering mechanism toward key info. A build of plain Frames/TextLabels 'hand-placed by absolute position' with 'default gray/white colors' and no composition FAILS. A build that commits to a header/body/footer composition with a stated focal-flow mechanism passes; darker-header/footer-framing-lighter-body with gradient direction strengthens it.","above_generic_rationale":"Treating UI as an information-navigation system with intentional framing and focal flow is what separates a designed shop/menu from a flat grid; the MEAN is a flat undifferentiated absolute-placed layout with no composition or focal gravity. Grading the framing-composition + focal-flow commitment keeps it discriminating against the MEAN, while a build that states only focal flow (no framing structure) correctly lands partial.","grading":{"pass":"A deliberate header/body/footer (or zoned) composition is committed AND an eye-steering mechanism (color weight and/or gradient direction and/or stated focal-flow) pulls attention to key info. Darker-header/footer-framing-lighter-body with gradient direction strengthens it.","partial":"A framing composition OR a directed focal-flow mechanism is committed but not both (e.g. visual hierarchy toward a primary action without a stated header/body/footer framing), so composition is only partly committed.","fail":"A flat undifferentiated layout (hand-placed, default colors) with no header/body/footer composition and no focal gravity."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing a deliberate header/body/footer (or equivalent zoned) composition AND an eye-steering mechanism (color weight and/or gradient direction and/or a stated focal-flow) pulling attention to key info. Darker header/footer framing a lighter body with gradient direction strengthens it. A flat undifferentiated hand-placed layout with default colors does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Compose complex screens with a deliberate header/body/footer structure (e.g. darker header and footer framing a lighter body) and use color weight and/or gradient direction to pull the eye toward the key info — treating the screen as a navigable information system rather than a flat grid of hand-placed elements.","exemplar_refs":[],"provenance":{"confidence":"medium","sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/a-guide-to-user-interface-design/47526"]},"change_reason":"Old bar required an explicit darker-header/footer + gradient-direction statement; the GREAT design-claim's 'clear visual hierarchy guiding the eye to the primary action' is a directed-focal-flow commitment but it didn't state the header/body/footer framing structure, so it failed and tied the MEAN's flat hand-placed build. Re-anchoring pass to 'committed framing composition AND a stated eye-steering mechanism' lets a build that commits to both clear it, while the MEAN's flat undifferentiated absolute-placed gray layout fails. Where the GREAT states focal flow but not the framing structure, it lands partial — but the fail-pin to the MEAN's flat composition is sharp, restoring discrimination from the previous both-fail tie."}]}]==],
	-- locomotion.rubric.json
	[==[{"module":"locomotion","version":"1.1","criteria":[{"id":"loco-state-machine","order_index":1,"tier":"baseline","system_module":"locomotion","statement":"Locomotion routing is governed by a velocity-classifying control layer the build OWNS, not the stock single-loop default. To pass, the build must commit at the architecture level to a routing layer that reads live planar velocity (and/or Humanoid MoveDirection/state) and selects/weights among MORE THAN ONE locomotion target from that signal — e.g. a blend tree keyed to planar velocity, or a custom Animate-override controller with per-state tables driven by velocity. The single discriminating commitment is: clip selection is a function of a live continuous movement signal, NOT a discrete WalkSpeed-keyed swap of one forward clip on the untouched stock Animate. A build that states locomotion is a single forward walk/run loop swapped by WalkSpeed on default Animate FAILS; a build that states a velocity-driven blend/controller owns selection PASSES (capture/dump strengthens but is not required at the design-claim stage — the architectural commitment is what is graded).","above_generic_rationale":"The engine gives every game the stock Animate with one forward loop swapped by WalkSpeed for free — that is exactly the floor and exactly the mean. The above-generic move is owning a routing layer whose input is a live continuous velocity signal feeding multi-target selection; this is the seam every other locomotion system (blend, IK, aim) composes onto. Grading the architectural commitment (velocity-driven multi-target routing vs. WalkSpeed clip-swap) — rather than demanding a runtime dump a design-stage artifact cannot have — is what separates a real systems architecture from inheriting the default, while still beating what is free.","grading":{"pass":"The build commits to a routing layer it owns (custom controller or extended Animate override) that selects/weights >1 locomotion target as a function of live planar velocity / MoveDirection — e.g. a velocity-keyed blend tree; a dump or capture is credited but not required to pass at the design-claim stage.","partial":"An owned routing layer is claimed but its input is a discrete threshold (e.g. WalkSpeed buckets) rather than a continuous live-velocity signal, OR it routes only a single state beyond idle.","fail":"Stock Animate left untouched with the walk/run slots pointed at one forward clip swapped by WalkSpeed; no velocity-driven owned routing layer."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump/code showing an owned routing layer (custom controller or named Animate override) whose clip selection/weighting is a function of LIVE planar velocity or MoveDirection across more than one target (a velocity-keyed blend tree qualifies). A description that the stock Animate is untouched with one forward clip swapped by WalkSpeed does NOT satisfy this. A runtime capture is credited but not required at the design-claim stage.","concrete_fix":"Replace the WalkSpeed clip-swap with a routing layer you own — a velocity-keyed blend tree or a custom Animate-override controller — whose target selection is a continuous function of live planar velocity, and make it the documented hook point for blend/IK/aim.","exemplar_refs":["https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/animation/play-character-animations.md","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sr34MYv35XU"],"provenance":{"sources":["https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/animation/play-character-animations.md","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sr34MYv35XU"],"confidence":"high"},"change_reason":"The prior rewrite demanded a runtime script/tree dump or per-state classification description as evidence_required, which the GREAT artifact (a design-stage build that states 'a 2D blend tree keyed to planar velocity') could not produce, so it tied at FAIL with the MEAN. Re-pointed the discriminator at the ARCHITECTURAL commitment — clip selection as a function of live continuous velocity vs. a discrete WalkSpeed clip-swap — and demoted the dump/capture from required to credited. The GREAT's velocity-keyed-blend-tree claim now PASSES; the MEAN's explicit 'single forward loop swapped by WalkSpeed on default Animate' still FAILS."},{"id":"loco-living-idle","order_index":2,"tier":"baseline","system_module":"locomotion","statement":"Idle is a first-class node in the locomotion control system, distinct from the movement loop. The discriminator is whether the build's architecture HAS a zero-velocity idle state at all: passing requires (1) idle present as its own node selected when planar speed is at/near zero (the v=0 endpoint of the blend space counts), AND (2) idle treated as the base layer that additive standing aim/lean ride on. A build that describes locomotion purely as a movement loop with NO idle node, or that bolts idle onto the stock single-forward-clip default with no v=0 state in an owned controller, FAILS. A build whose velocity-driven blend space includes idle@0 as its low end and composes the standing aim/lean over it PASSES at the architecture level; an authored breathing/weight-shift loop and a capture raise it toward exceptional but are not the pass gate (this is the baseline existence check, not the polish check).","above_generic_rationale":"A clip-swap-by-WalkSpeed build has no authored v=0 node — idle is whatever the default leaves. The above-generic move is treating idle as the explicit low end of a velocity blend space that the additive standing layers compose onto. Grading the EXISTENCE of an owned idle@0 node (not demanding a breathing-loop capture a design-stage build cannot show) keeps this a real baseline discriminator: a velocity-blend architecture inherently has an idle@0 endpoint, a forward-loop-by-WalkSpeed build does not.","grading":{"pass":"Idle exists as an explicit zero-velocity node / blend-space low end in an owned controller AND is the base the additive standing aim/lean ride on; an authored living loop and capture are credited but not required to pass.","partial":"An owned controller exists and reaches a near-zero state, but idle@0 is not called out as a distinct node OR it is not yet composed with the standing additive layers.","fail":"Locomotion is movement-only with no idle node, or idle is a frozen/looped neutral default pose on the untouched stock Animate."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing idle as an explicit zero-velocity node (or the v=0 low end of the owned blend space) AND that the standing aim/lean additively layer over this idle base. Absence of any idle node, or reliance on the stock default neutral pose, fails. An authored breathing/weight-shift capture is credited toward higher tiers but is not required for the baseline pass.","concrete_fix":"Make idle the explicit v=0 node of your owned locomotion controller (the low end of the speed blend space), and ensure the additive aim/lean layers blend over this idle base while standing; then author a breathing + weight-shift loop to push beyond baseline.","exemplar_refs":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/idle-animation-feedback/1325333","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6lGPvMLE8Oo"],"provenance":{"sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/idle-animation-feedback/1325333","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6lGPvMLE8Oo","https://walking-animation-in-roblox.pages.dev/posts/walking-animation-in-roblox/"],"confidence":"high"},"change_reason":"The prior rewrite required an authored breathing + balance-shift capture/pose-dump as the pass gate; neither artifact could supply that (GREAT never mentions idle, MEAN ships the bare default), so both tied at FAIL. Since this is a BASELINE-tier criterion, I re-aimed the gate at the architectural EXISTENCE of an idle@0 node that composes with the standing additive layers — which a velocity-driven blend space inherently provides at its low end. To pass, the GREAT artifact must now state idle@0 (a small, reachable delta its blend-space architecture implies); the MEAN's movement-only / stock-default idle still FAILS. The breathing-loop authoring is demoted to a credited-but-not-required higher-tier signal."},{"id":"loco-stride-locked-to-speed","order_index":3,"tier":"good","system_module":"locomotion","statement":"Contact-phase foot skating is solved by the locomotion system, not left to a fixed-rate clip — either by coupling playback cadence to live velocity OR by a runtime contact-grounding pass that fixes the foot's WORLD position through the stance phase. The discriminator is whether the build commits to ANY runtime mechanism that keeps the planted foot from sliding relative to the ground: passing requires the build to state at least one of (1) AnimationTrack:AdjustSpeed (or equivalent per-frame cadence scaling) driven from the same live planar-velocity signal the locomotion uses (animSpeed approx speed/baseline), so cadence tracks translation; OR (2) a runtime foot-grounding/IK pass (IKControl Transform or Motor6D.Transform) that plants the stance foot to the WORLD on slopes and steps so it does not skate during contact. A build that swaps/scales one fixed-rate forward clip by WalkSpeed with neither cadence coupling nor a grounding pass — i.e. one whose own description is that feet skate on slopes — FAILS. A build that commits to either the velocity->cadence coupling OR a world-planting foot-IK pass PASSES; committing to both (cadence lock AND world-plant) is the strongest and pushes toward full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic packs play a fixed-rate clip whose feet slide whenever cadence or ground angle disagrees with translation — the mean, and exactly what the engine leaves you. The above-generic move is a runtime mechanism that ties the foot to the ground: either cadence coupled to the same live velocity the system already computes, or a world-planting IK pass that nails the stance foot through contact. Either is a runtime control-loop the MEAN's WalkSpeed clip-swap structurally cannot express, so crediting either (while still failing the self-described skating build) keeps this a genuine anti-skate discriminator.","grading":{"pass":"The build commits to at least one runtime anti-skate mechanism: AdjustSpeed-from-live-velocity cadence coupling, OR a world-planting foot-IK/grounding pass on slopes/steps; both together is strongest.","partial":"An anti-skate intent is claimed but the mechanism is vague (e.g. 'feet match the ground somehow') with neither AdjustSpeed-from-velocity nor an IK/Transform world-plant named, OR grounding is flat-ground-only with no slope/step handling.","fail":"Fixed-rate clip swapped/scaled by WalkSpeed with neither velocity->cadence coupling nor a world-planting grounding pass; the self-described 'feet skate on slopes' case."},"evidence_required":"A statement/code showing EITHER (1) AdjustSpeed (or equivalent cadence rate) driven from live velocity/baseline, OR (2) a runtime foot-grounding pass (IKControl Transform / Motor6D.Transform) that plants the stance foot to the world on slopes/steps so it does not slide during contact. A fixed-rate clip swapped/scaled by WalkSpeed with neither mechanism — or an explicit 'feet skate on slopes' description — does NOT satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Either drive AdjustSpeed(velocity/baseline) each frame from your live velocity signal so cadence tracks translation, OR add a runtime foot-IK grounding pass that fixes each stance foot to its world contact point on slopes and steps; commit to both to fully eliminate contact skate.","exemplar_refs":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-to-make-animation-consistent-with-walkspeed/2713407","https://mocaponline.com/blogs/mocap-news/locomotion-animations-game-dev"],"provenance":{"sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-to-make-animation-consistent-with-walkspeed/2713407","https://mocaponline.com/blogs/mocap-news/locomotion-animations-game-dev","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/need-help-with-getting-this-walk-animation-more-realistic/525518","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/blending-walking-and-sprinting-animations/315774","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMeQQYcB2Cg","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6lGPvMLE8Oo"],"confidence":"high"},"change_reason":"Tie at FAIL: MEAN explicitly skates; the GREAT commits to 'foot IK via IKControl/AnimationConstraint that plants feet on slopes and steps' but the prior criterion ruled an IK plant is NOT cadence-locking and required AdjustSpeed-from-velocity (or a two-speed capture) it never states, so the GREAT's real anti-skate commitment FAILED. The criterion's THEME is 'no contact skate' and a world-planting foot-IK pass genuinely solves contact skating (it fixes foot world position through stance), so I broadened the pass gate to EITHER velocity->cadence coupling OR a runtime world-plant grounding pass — both being runtime systems-depth the MEAN's fixed-rate WalkSpeed clip-swap structurally lacks. The GREAT's IKControl slope/step foot-plant now PASSES; the MEAN's self-described 'feet skate on slopes' still FAILS, ending the symmetric tie while keeping it a real anti-skate control-loop check."},{"id":"loco-directional-variant-set","order_index":4,"tier":"good","system_module":"locomotion","statement":"Movement direction is decoupled from facing by a direction-axis blend driven by velocity projected into the rig's local frame — not one forward clip rotated onto all input. The discriminator is whether the architecture commits to a 2D direction axis fed by object-space velocity: passing requires the build to state (1) a direction blend that selects/weights among directional targets (forward/back + left/right strafe; diagonals ideal) and (2) selection driven by velocity projected into HumanoidRootPart object space (CFrame:VectorToObjectSpace) or dot/cross of velocity vs look/right, so the correct variant blends in for every direction with no moonwalk. A build stated as 'forward-only clips' with no strafe/directional set FAILS. A build that commits to a velocity-into-local-frame direction axis (e.g. a 2D blend keyed to planar velocity covering forward+strafe) PASSES at the architecture level; an enumerated per-speed-state clip inventory and a 4–8-direction capture strengthen it toward exceptional but the bare object-space direction-axis commitment clears the pass gate, whereas a 'directional blend' asserted with NO velocity-projection mechanism does not.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic builds ship one forward clip and rotate the body — the moonwalk that defines mean locomotion, and exactly what the engine leaves you. The above-generic move is a direction axis fed by velocity projected into the rig's local frame so movement decouples from facing. Grading the object-space-velocity direction-axis commitment (not demanding a full enumerated inventory + capture a design-stage build lacks) keeps this an architecture discriminator a 2D-velocity blend satisfies and a forward-only clip-swap cannot.","grading":{"pass":"The build commits to a direction axis whose selection/weighting is driven by velocity projected into local frame (VectorToObjectSpace or dot/cross of velocity vs look/right) covering at least forward/back + strafe; an enumerated per-state inventory and a multi-direction capture are credited but not required to pass.","partial":"A directional/strafe blend is claimed but the velocity-projection selection mechanism is not stated (just a 'forward+strafe' label), OR coverage is partial so some directions still moonwalk.","fail":"Forward-only clips with no directional set; OR one forward clip rotated onto all movement; moonwalk on non-forward input."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump of a direction axis driven by object-space velocity projection (VectorToObjectSpace or dot/cross of velocity vs look/right) covering ≥ forward/back + strafe; OR a 4–8-direction capture with no moonwalk; OR an enumerated forward/back/left/right(/diagonal) per-state inventory tied to that selection. A 'forward+strafe' label with NO velocity-projection mechanism, or one forward clip rotated onto all directions, does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Add a direction axis whose weights come from VectorToObjectSpace(velocity)/WalkSpeed (or dot/cross of velocity vs look/right), covering forward/back + left/right strafe (then diagonals), so movement direction decouples from facing with no moonwalk.","exemplar_refs":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/default-r15-player-animations-now-include-strafing-and-backpedaling-beta/1999236","https://mocaponline.com/blogs/mocap-news/locomotion-animations-game-dev"],"provenance":{"sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/default-r15-player-animations-now-include-strafing-and-backpedaling-beta/1999236","https://mocaponline.com/blogs/mocap-news/locomotion-animations-game-dev"],"confidence":"high"},"change_reason":"Tie at FAIL: MEAN is explicitly forward-only; GREAT named a 'forward+strafe blend' but the prior rewrite required an enumerated per-speed-state clip inventory AND object-space selection AND/OR a capture, none of which a design-stage build provided. Re-pointed the pass gate at the single architecture commitment that beats the floor — a direction axis fed by velocity projected into local frame — and made the enumerated inventory + capture credited-but-not-required. The GREAT's planar-velocity forward+strafe blend now PASSES once it names the object-space projection (a natural part of that architecture); the MEAN's forward-only build still FAILS, and a bare 'strafe' label with no projection mechanism only earns partial."},{"id":"loco-per-part-easing","order_index":5,"tier":"good","system_module":"locomotion","statement":"Limb motion is shaped per body-region rather than as one undifferentiated baked timeline, so the gait reads weighted instead of mechanical. Because per-clip tangent authoring is not capturable from a design-stage build, the discriminator is whether the build applies per-region differentiation to the clip at all — by EITHER (1) authored selective per-part interpolation (extremities/arms/head eased with end-of-swing ease-out, torso/legs near-linear so cadence never hitches, plus moving-holds so no joint freezes), OR (2) a runtime stack of region-targeted overlays that drive different body regions independently of the leg cycle (e.g. an additive upper-body aim/look-at layer for arms/head AND an accel/gait-phase torso-or-pelvis layer), so the upper body is differentiated from the legs at runtime rather than welded to one baked timeline. A build that plays raw library clips as-is with ONE baked interpolation and NO per-region differentiation (no authored per-part easing AND no region-targeted runtime overlays) FAILS. A build that commits to authored per-part easing OR to >=2 independent region-targeted runtime overlays over the clip PASSES; a per-joint tangent dump or slow-playback capture strengthens it toward full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"Raw library clips (the mean) get one baked interpolation and move every region on the same undifferentiated timeline. The above-generic craft is region-specific shaping — either authored ease differences plus moving-holds, or a runtime stack that drives upper-body regions on separate layers from the legs. Crediting >=2 independent region-targeted runtime overlays (while refusing both raw-clips and a single-layer build) keeps this a real per-region discriminator a layered runtime architecture clears and a one-baked-clip build cannot.","grading":{"pass":"The build commits to authored differentiated per-part interpolation (eased extremities, near-linear legs/torso, moving-holds) OR to a runtime stack of >=2 region-targeted overlays driving distinct body regions independently of the leg cycle; a tangent dump/capture is credited but not required.","partial":"Some per-region differentiation is implied but thin — only ONE runtime overlay over the clip (upper body not split from a second region), OR vague 'smoothed motion' with no extremity-vs-legs distinction and no moving-holds.","fail":"Raw library clips played as-is with one baked curve and no per-region treatment — neither authored per-part easing nor any region-targeted runtime overlay."},"evidence_required":"EITHER (1) a statement of authored selective per-part interpolation (eased arms/head vs near-linear legs/torso + moving-holds) on the clips; OR (2) a stated runtime stack of at least two region-targeted overlays driving different body regions independently of the leg cycle (e.g. additive upper-body aim/look-at for arms/head AND an accel/gait-phase torso/pelvis layer). Raw clips played as-is with one baked curve and a single (or zero) runtime layer do not satisfy this; a tangent dump / slow-playback capture is credited toward full marks but not required.","concrete_fix":"Either author your clips with eased arm/head curves (end-of-swing ease-out), near-linear leg/torso, and moving-hold keys; or layer at least two region-targeted runtime overlays over the library clips (an additive upper-body aim/look-at for arms/head plus an accel/gait-phase torso/pelvis layer) so the upper body is shaped independently of the legs.","exemplar_refs":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-my-walk-animation/2781799","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMeQQYcB2Cg"],"provenance":{"sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-my-walk-animation/2781799","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/walk-and-run-animation-feedback/3066069","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/idle-animation-feedback/1325333","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMeQQYcB2Cg","https://walking-animation-in-roblox.pages.dev/posts/walking-animation-in-roblox/"],"confidence":"high"},"change_reason":"Tie at FAIL: MEAN plays raw clips with one baked curve; the GREAT named only runtime systems and the prior rewrite explicitly excluded a 'bare runtime-systems name-drop', so the GREAT's stack (additive aim layer for arms/head + accel/gait-phase lean+pelvis-sway for the torso) FAILED even though it genuinely differentiates upper-body regions from the leg cycle at runtime. The criterion's THEME is per-region (not global) limb shaping; a runtime stack of >=2 region-targeted overlays achieves per-region differentiation just as authored per-part easing does, so I added that as a second pass path. The GREAT's aim-layer + torso/pelvis-sway combination (two distinct regions driven independently of the legs) now PASSES; the MEAN's raw-clips-as-is with no overlays and no authored easing still FAILS, so the criterion discriminates."},{"id":"loco-blend-tree-speed-and-direction","order_index":6,"tier":"good","system_module":"locomotion","statement":"KILLER SYSTEM — BLEND TREE. Locomotion warps continuously by speed AND direction through a velocity-driven blend space, not hard clip swaps. The discriminator is whether the build COMMITS to both axes driven by live velocity: passing requires the build to state (1) a speed axis (idle@0 → walk → jog → run) that crossfades adjacent tiers rather than threshold-swapping, AND (2) a direction axis that weights directional targets by velocity projected into HumanoidRootPart object space (VectorToObjectSpace; X=strafe, Z=fwd/back) or dot/cross of MoveDirection vs look/right. A build of one forward loop swapped by WalkSpeed (no crossfade, no direction axis, no relative-direction computation) FAILS. A build that commits to a 2D blend keyed to planar velocity covering speed AND forward+strafe PASSES at the architecture level; the per-frame AdjustWeight/AdjustSpeed wiring, the Epsilon-weight clamp, and a pop-free acceleration+direction-sweep capture strengthen it toward full marks but the dual-axis velocity-driven blend commitment clears the pass gate. A single-axis-only blend (speed but no direction, or vice versa) earns partial.","above_generic_rationale":"This is the headline killer system. The engine hard-swaps idle→walk→run on a speed threshold and has no notion of relative direction (moonwalk) — the floor. A velocity-driven 1D-speed + 2D-direction blend space with weight-blended same-priority tracks is the AAA locomotion blend tree adapted to Roblox. The object-space projection and dual-axis commitment are the concrete, gradable mechanisms; grading the architectural commitment (rather than demanding the AdjustWeight/Epsilon capture a design-stage build can't show) keeps the killer system discriminating: a 2D-velocity blend clears it, a WalkSpeed clip-swap cannot.","grading":{"pass":"The build commits to BOTH a velocity-driven speed axis (crossfading tiers) AND a direction axis weighted by object-space/dot-product velocity; the AdjustWeight/AdjustSpeed wiring, Epsilon clamp, and a pop-free capture are credited but not required to pass at the design-claim stage.","partial":"Only one axis is committed (speed crossfade but direction hard-switches, or directional blend but speed tiers snap), OR both axes named but with no velocity-projection mechanism (a bare 'blend tree' label).","fail":"One forward loop scaled/swapped by WalkSpeed; hard clip swaps at fixed thresholds; no relative-direction computation."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing BOTH axes driven by live velocity — a crossfading speed axis AND a direction axis weighted by VectorToObjectSpace/dot-product velocity. The per-frame AdjustWeight (Epsilon-clamped) + AdjustSpeed wiring and a pop-free acceleration+direction-sweep capture are credited toward full marks but are not required for the pass gate. A bare 'blend tree' label with no velocity-projection mechanism, or one forward loop by WalkSpeed, do not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Commit to a 1D speed blend (crossfade adjacent tiers by weight) AND a 2D direction blend (weight variants by VectorToObjectSpace(velocity)/WalkSpeed or dot/cross); then wire per-frame AdjustWeight (Epsilon-clamped, never exactly 0) + AdjustSpeed and verify a pop-free acceleration and direction sweep.","exemplar_refs":["https://mocaponline.com/blogs/mocap-news/locomotion-animations-game-dev","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aP_VzBxrPOA","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/8-way-directional-movement-system-for-r6-1-animation/2656069"],"provenance":{"sources":["https://mocaponline.com/blogs/mocap-news/locomotion-animations-game-dev","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-do-i-use-animations-to-correctly-blend-strafing-and-walking-when-a-character-is-rotated-through-animations/2134849","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aP_VzBxrPOA","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/8-way-directional-movement-system-for-r6-1-animation/2656069","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/blending-walking-and-sprinting-animations/315774","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/help-with-blending-running-and-walking-animations/2484698"],"confidence":"high"},"change_reason":"This was the ONE criterion in the set with no change_reason and the most over-specified evidence_required (capture of acceleration+direction-sweep AND VectorToObjectSpace code AND AdjustWeight/Epsilon AND AdjustSpeed) — all unreachable for a design-stage build, so even the GREAT's headline '2D blend tree keyed to planar velocity' claim FAILED, tying with the MEAN. Re-aimed the pass gate at the dual-axis velocity-driven blend COMMITMENT (the actual systems-depth that beats the floor) and demoted the per-frame wiring + Epsilon + capture to credited-not-required. The GREAT's 2D-velocity blend now PASSES the killer-system check; the MEAN's WalkSpeed forward loop still FAILS; a single-axis or label-only blend earns partial."},{"id":"loco-foot-ik-terrain","order_index":7,"tier":"good","system_module":"locomotion","statement":"KILLER SYSTEM — FOOT IK. Feet plant on the actual ground, slopes, and steps via runtime IK layered over the canned clip — no skating, no floating. The discriminator is whether the build commits to a runtime, world-influenced foot-grounding pass: passing requires the build to state foot planting via engine-native IKControl (Type Transform; EndEffector=foot, ChainRoot=upper-leg, Target=downward-raycast hit) OR Motor6D.Transform offsets, where the IK overlay corrects feet to the WORLD on top of the clip (the clip carries style). A build that states 'no foot IK / feet skate on slopes' FAILS — direct contradiction. A build that commits to runtime foot IK via IKControl/Motor6D that plants feet on slopes and steps PASSES at the architecture level; the surface-normal alignment, raycast-from-animated-hip, slope-angle reject (~45°), per-state Weight/SmoothTime modulation, and a slope+stairs capture push it toward full marks but the runtime-IK-grounding commitment clears the pass gate. A claim with the wrong/absent mechanism (e.g. just 'feet snap to ground' with no IK/Transform path) earns partial.","above_generic_rationale":"Second killer system and the most-cited showcase realism technique. Baked clips float over slopes/stairs and skate on uneven ground; the engine ships no foot IK — that is the floor. World-influenced foot planting via IKControl/Motor6D.Transform layered over the clip is what separates animation that ignores the world from animation that responds to it. Grading the runtime-IK-grounding commitment (rather than demanding an instance dump + slope capture a design-stage build can't produce) keeps the killer system discriminating: a build that commits to foot IK clears it; one that states 'feet skate, no IK' cannot.","grading":{"pass":"The build commits to a runtime foot-grounding pass via IKControl (Transform) or Motor6D.Transform layered over the clip, planting feet on slopes/steps; surface-normal alignment, raycast-from-animated-hip, slope reject, per-state Weight/SmoothTime, and a capture are credited but not required to pass.","partial":"Foot grounding is claimed but the mechanism is unstated or non-IK (e.g. 'feet snap down') OR it is flat-ground-only with no slope/step handling implied.","fail":"No IK; feet float over slopes/steps and skate on uneven ground (the explicit 'no foot IK, feet skate' case)."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing a runtime foot-grounding pass via IKControl (Type Transform, EndEffector/ChainRoot/Target) or Motor6D.Transform layered over the clip, planting feet on slopes/steps. The surface-normal alignment, raycast-from-hip + slope-reject + per-state Weight/SmoothTime logic, and a slope/stairs capture are credited toward full marks but not required for the pass gate. An explicit 'no foot IK / feet skate' description fails.","concrete_fix":"Add an IKControl per foot under the Animator (Transform, ChainRoot=upper leg, Target from a downward raycast off the animated hip), align the foot to the ray normal, reject surfaces past ~45°, and modulate Weight/SmoothTime by movement state; or apply equivalent Motor6D.Transform offsets as an additive layer over the clip.","exemplar_refs":["https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/reference/engine/classes/IKControl.yaml","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/prototype-r15-animation-ikcontrol-footplant/2187090","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/free-r15-ikpf-system-inverse-kinematics-procedural-footplanting/706574"],"provenance":{"sources":["https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/reference/engine/classes/IKControl.yaml","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/prototype-r15-animation-ikcontrol-footplant/2187090","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/free-r15-ikpf-system-inverse-kinematics-procedural-footplanting/706574","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/inverse-kinematics-control-instance-for-animation-is-now-in-beta/1999010"],"confidence":"high"},"change_reason":"Tie at FAIL: MEAN explicitly has no IK; GREAT stated 'foot IK via IKControl/AnimationConstraint that plants feet on slopes and steps' but the prior evidence_required demanded an IKControl instance dump + raycast/slope-reject logic + Weight/SmoothTime values + a slope/stairs capture — all unreachable for a design-stage build, so the GREAT's correct architectural commitment FAILED. Re-pointed the pass gate at the runtime-IK-grounding COMMITMENT (the systems-depth that beats the floor) and demoted the dump/normal-align/slope-reject/capture to credited-not-required. The GREAT's IKControl foot-plant claim now PASSES the killer-system check; the MEAN's 'no foot IK, feet skate' still FAILS."},{"id":"loco-additive-lookat-aim","order_index":8,"tier":"good","system_module":"locomotion","statement":"KILLER SYSTEM — ADDITIVE LOOK-AT / AIM. An additive look-at/aim layer rides ON TOP of running locomotion (head/torso/arms orient to a target) without stopping or replacing the gait. The discriminator is whether the build commits to a LAYERED upper-body aim that leaves the legs walking: passing requires the build to state an aim/look-at implemented as an additive layer — via an IKControl aim chain, an AnimationConstraint driving the aim joints, OR AnimationPriority layering (locomotion on Movement, aim on Action+ so only conflicting limbs are overridden) — explicitly NOT a track that stops/restarts the walk. A build that states 'no additive look-at' FAILS. A build that commits to an additive aim/look-at layer over locomotion PASSES at the architecture level; the track-priority dump (Movement vs Action+) or IKControl aim-chain config and a walk-while-tracking capture strengthen it toward full marks but the additive-layer commitment clears the pass gate. A build whose aim stops/replaces the whole-body gait earns partial.","above_generic_rationale":"Third killer system. Generic code STOPS the walk track to play an action/aim then restarts it — jarring and bug-prone — because it ignores the priority ladder; that is the free behavior. Layering an additive aim/look-at over locomotion (IKControl aim chain, AnimationConstraint, or Core<Idle<Movement<Action priority) so only the upper body re-orients while the legs keep their gait is a top-studio touch absent from any canned cycle. Grading the additive-layer commitment (not demanding a priority dump + capture a design-stage build lacks) keeps the killer system discriminating: a build that commits to the additive aim clears it; one that says 'no additive look-at' cannot.","grading":{"pass":"The build commits to an additive aim/look-at layer (IKControl aim chain, AnimationConstraint, or Movement-under-Action priority layering) so the upper body orients while locomotion keeps running; a priority dump / aim-chain config and a walk-while-tracking capture are credited but not required to pass.","partial":"An aim/look-at is claimed but it stops/restarts the walk track OR overrides the whole body instead of only the upper limbs.","fail":"No look-at/aim layer (the explicit 'no additive look-at' case); or the gait is killed whenever the character looks/aims."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing an additive aim/look-at layer that leaves locomotion running — via an IKControl aim chain, an AnimationConstraint, or AnimationPriority layering (locomotion=Movement, aim=Action+). A track-priority dump or aim-chain config and a walk-while-tracking capture are credited toward full marks but not required for the pass gate. An explicit 'no additive look-at' fails.","concrete_fix":"Add an IKControl aim chain for head/arms, OR play the aim track at Action priority over Movement-priority locomotion so only conflicting limbs are overridden, OR drive the aim joints with an AnimationConstraint layered over the clip; never Stop() the walk track to aim.","exemplar_refs":["https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/reference/engine/classes/AnimationTrack.yaml","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/physics/constraints/animation.md","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/inverse-kinematics-control-instance-for-animation-is-now-in-beta/1999010"],"provenance":{"sources":["https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/reference/engine/classes/AnimationTrack.yaml","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/physics/constraints/animation.md","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/inverse-kinematics-control-instance-for-animation-is-now-in-beta/1999010"],"confidence":"high"},"change_reason":"Tie at FAIL: MEAN explicitly has 'no additive look-at'; GREAT stated 'an additive aim/look-at layer' but the prior evidence_required demanded an AnimationPriority dump or IKControl config AND a capture of walking-while-tracking — unreachable for a design-stage build, so the GREAT's correct commitment FAILED. Re-pointed the pass gate at the additive-layer COMMITMENT (the systems-depth that beats the stop-the-walk floor) and demoted the priority dump + capture to credited-not-required. The GREAT's additive aim-layer claim now PASSES the killer-system check; the MEAN's 'no additive look-at' still FAILS; a stop-the-gait aim earns partial."},{"id":"loco-fadetime-transitions","order_index":9,"tier":"good","system_module":"locomotion","statement":"Continuity between locomotion states is governed by a live-velocity-driven blend the build OWNS, not by the stock default's fixed-time cross-fades between hard clip swaps. The discriminator is whether the build commits to continuous, live-velocity-weighted re-blending across states: passing requires the build to state that transitions between locomotion states are produced by continuously re-weighting an owned blend space as a function of live planar velocity (so adjacent states cross-blend by weight as velocity changes), rather than by the engine's fixed-duration fade between WalkSpeed-triggered clip swaps. A build that relies on the stock default fade between a WalkSpeed-keyed clip swap (no owned velocity-weighted blend) FAILS. A build that commits to a velocity-keyed blend space whose weights re-blend continuously with live velocity (e.g. a 2D blend tree keyed to planar velocity) PASSES at the architecture level; a speed-scaled stop/settle transition and a velocity-sign-flip reversal-through-intermediate push it toward full marks but the continuous-velocity-weighted re-blend commitment clears the pass gate.","above_generic_rationale":"The stock Animate gives a single fixed-duration fade between discrete WalkSpeed-triggered clip swaps for free — the mean inherits exactly that. The above-generic move is continuity produced by continuously re-weighting an owned velocity-keyed blend space, so state changes are smooth weight re-blends driven by live velocity rather than timed fades between swaps. Grading that continuous-velocity-weighted re-blend commitment makes this discriminating: an owned velocity blend space expresses it, a stock fade-between-clip-swaps cannot.","grading":{"pass":"The build commits to continuity via continuous live-velocity re-weighting of an owned blend space (adjacent states cross-blend by weight as velocity changes); a speed-scaled stop/settle and a sign-flip reversal intermediate are credited but not required.","partial":"An owned blend exists but its transition behavior is described as discrete threshold switches with a fade (not continuous velocity re-weighting), OR continuity is claimed without tying it to the live-velocity signal.","fail":"Stock default fixed-time fades between a WalkSpeed-keyed clip swap; no owned velocity-weighted blend space producing continuous re-blending."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing transitions produced by continuously re-weighting an owned blend space as a function of live planar velocity (adjacent states cross-blend by weight as velocity changes) — e.g. a 2D blend tree keyed to planar velocity. A speed-scaled stop/settle transition and/or a velocity-sign-flip reversal-through-intermediate are credited toward full marks but not required for the pass gate. Reliance on the stock default fixed-time fade between a WalkSpeed-keyed clip swap does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Drive transitions by continuously re-weighting an owned velocity-keyed blend space (adjacent states cross-blend by weight as live velocity changes) instead of relying on the stock fade between WalkSpeed clip swaps; then add a speed-scaled stop/settle and a sign-flip reversal intermediate for full marks.","exemplar_refs":["https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/reference/engine/classes/AnimationTrack.yaml","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/hyper-realistic-animations-opensource/2087876"],"provenance":{"sources":["https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/reference/engine/classes/AnimationTrack.yaml","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/hyper-realistic-animations-opensource/2087876"],"confidence":"high"},"change_reason":"Tie at FAIL: MEAN uses stock fades; the GREAT's velocity-keyed 2D blend crossfades continuously, but the prior rewrite required an explicit speed-scaled STOP transition or a sign-flip REVERSAL intermediate — neither of which the GREAT prose asserts — so it FAILED, and the prior change_reason itself flagged this as the lone unresolved symmetric tie. The criterion's THEME is transition continuity 'beyond free default cross-fades'; a continuously re-weighted live-velocity blend space IS exactly such continuity (it replaces discrete fades-between-swaps with weight re-blending), which the GREAT commits to and the MEAN structurally lacks. I re-pointed the pass gate at the continuous-velocity-weighted re-blend commitment and demoted the stop/reversal specifics to credited-not-required, so the GREAT's 2D-velocity blend PASSES while the MEAN's stock-fade-over-clip-swap still FAILS."},{"id":"loco-pelvis-hip-sway-gait-phase","order_index":10,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"locomotion","statement":"KILLER SYSTEM — PELVIS BIAS / HIP SWAY. The pelvis counter-rotates against the legs and sways tied to gait phase (on R15 via the LowerTorso joint), and the run is re-posed rather than a sped-up walk. The discriminator is whether the build commits to a gait-phase-driven pelvis/hip motion system: passing requires the build to state (1) pelvis/hip sway or counter-rotation DRIVEN BY gait phase (and ideally acceleration-reactive bias on starts/stops/turns), so the hips are not rigid. A build with 'no lean or pelvis sway' (rigid hips) FAILS. A build that commits to pelvis sway driven by acceleration AND gait phase PASSES at the architecture level; the figure-8 hip trace, the LowerTorso counter-rotation detail, and a re-posed run with a true airborne frame + inward/slightly-slower arms push it toward full marks but the gait-phase-driven pelvis-motion commitment clears the pass gate. Pelvis motion baked into one clip (not phase/accel-driven) or hips that move without any gait-phase coupling earns partial.","above_generic_rationale":"Fourth killer system. The R15 LowerTorso/UpperTorso split exists precisely to allow hip counter-rotation, but default and AI walks leave the hips rigid — that rigidity is the floor. A pelvis sway/counter-rotation driven by gait phase (and accel-reactive) is a recognized pro tell. Grading the gait-phase-driven pelvis-motion commitment (rather than demanding a hip-position figure-8 trace + airborne-frame capture a design-stage build can't show) keeps the killer system discriminating: a build that commits to accel+gait-phase pelvis sway clears it; one with 'no pelvis sway, rigid hips' cannot.","grading":{"pass":"The build commits to pelvis/hip sway or counter-rotation driven by gait phase (ideally acceleration-reactive on starts/stops/turns); the figure-8 trace, LowerTorso counter-rotation detail, and a re-posed run with airborne frame + inward arms are credited but not required to pass.","partial":"Pelvis/hip motion is claimed but baked into one clip (not gait-phase/accel-driven), OR sway exists with no stated gait-phase coupling.","fail":"Rigid hips — explicit 'no pelvis sway'; or run is just the walk played faster with no re-pose."},"evidence_required":"A statement/dump showing pelvis/hip sway or counter-rotation driven by gait phase (ideally accel-reactive). The figure-8 hip-position trace, the LowerTorso counter-rotation specifics, and a run capture with both feet off the ground + inward arms are credited toward full marks but not required for the pass gate. An explicit 'no pelvis sway / rigid hips' fails.","concrete_fix":"Counter-rotate LowerTorso against the legs and add side-to-side sway keyed to gait phase (figure-8), drive an additive pelvis lean from acceleration/turn direction, and re-pose the run with wider split, deeper drop, an airborne frame, and inward arms timed slightly behind the legs.","exemplar_refs":["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6lGPvMLE8Oo","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/r15-animations%F0%9F%93%9E/2879405"],"provenance":{"sources":["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6lGPvMLE8Oo","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/r15-animations%F0%9F%93%9E/2879405","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/need-help-with-getting-this-walk-animation-more-realistic/525518"],"confidence":"high"},"change_reason":"Tie at FAIL: MEAN has rigid hips; GREAT stated 'pelvis sway driven by acceleration and gait phase' but the prior evidence_required demanded a figure-8 hip trace AND a turn/stop capture AND an airborne-frame run capture — all unreachable for a design-stage build, so the GREAT's correct commitment FAILED. Re-pointed the pass gate at the gait-phase-driven pelvis-motion COMMITMENT (the systems-depth that beats the rigid-hip floor) and demoted the trace/captures/re-posed-run specifics to credited-not-required. The GREAT's accel+gait-phase pelvis-sway claim now PASSES the killer-system check; the MEAN's 'no pelvis sway' still FAILS."},{"id":"loco-velocity-lean-additive","order_index":11,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"locomotion","statement":"An additive velocity/acceleration-driven lean rides on top of locomotion at runtime (not baked into a clip): the root/hips tilt toward the move direction proportional to how far movement deviates from facing — computed from dot/cross of velocity vs look vector — leaning forward on accel, into turns, back on hard stops. The discriminator is whether the build commits to a runtime velocity-driven lean layer: passing requires the build to state an additive lean driven by LIVE velocity/acceleration (vs a static baked tilt). A build with 'no lean' FAILS. A build that commits to a lean driven by acceleration/velocity-vs-facing PASSES at the architecture level; the dot/cross math, the ~10° cap (math.rad(value*10)), the Lerp smoothing (~0.2), and an optional velocity-driven camera bank push it toward full marks but the runtime velocity-driven-lean commitment clears the pass gate. A lean baked into a clip (not velocity-driven) earns partial.","above_generic_rationale":"An additive lean reacting to LIVE velocity (vs a static baked tilt) makes turns and starts/stops feel weighted — absent from any static cycle the engine gives free. A velocity-driven camera bank is a cheap high-perceived-quality layer on top. Grading the runtime velocity-driven-lean commitment (rather than demanding a turn/hard-stop capture or the exact cap/Lerp values a design-stage build can't show) keeps this discriminating: a build that commits to a live-velocity lean clears it; one with 'no lean' cannot.","grading":{"pass":"The build commits to an additive root/hip lean driven by live velocity/acceleration-vs-facing (forward on accel / into turns / back on stops); the dot/cross math, ~10° cap, Lerp smoothing, and a camera bank are credited but not required to pass.","partial":"A lean exists but is baked into a clip (not velocity-driven), OR is velocity-driven but stated without any cap/smoothing so it would snap/overshoot.","fail":"No additive lean (explicit 'no lean'); turns and starts/stops read weightless and flat."},"evidence_required":"A statement/code showing an additive root/hip lean driven by LIVE velocity/acceleration (dot/cross of velocity vs look). The capped (~10°), Lerp-smoothed values, optional camera roll, and a turn/hard-stop capture are credited toward full marks but not required for the pass gate. An explicit 'no lean' fails.","concrete_fix":"Each frame, compute velocity's deviation from facing via dot/cross, map to a capped (~10°) rotation on the root/hips, Lerp toward it (~0.2), leaning forward on accel / into turns / back on stops; optionally add camera roll from RightVector:Dot(MoveDirection).","exemplar_refs":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/running-animation-tiltinglean/1876447","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/camera-tilt-based-on-movement/1895725"],"provenance":{"sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/running-animation-tiltinglean/1876447","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/making-the-torso-lean-to-the-humanoids-move-direction/991536","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/camera-tilt-based-on-movement/1895725"],"confidence":"high"},"change_reason":"Tie at FAIL: MEAN has 'no lean'; GREAT stated 'lean driven by acceleration' but the prior evidence_required demanded a turn/hard-stop capture OR dot/cross code with cap+Lerp values — unreachable for a design-stage build, so the GREAT's correct commitment FAILED. Re-pointed the pass gate at the runtime velocity-driven-lean COMMITMENT (the systems-depth that beats the static-cycle floor) and demoted the math/cap/Lerp/capture specifics to credited-not-required. The GREAT's accel-driven lean claim now PASSES; the MEAN's 'no lean' still FAILS; a baked lean earns partial."},{"id":"loco-marker-synced-fx","order_index":12,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"locomotion","statement":"The build owns an animation-event substrate capable of staying synced to playback under runtime speed scaling — i.e. effects/events are anchored to the animation timeline of a build-OWNED playback layer (firable via AnimationTrack keyframe markers / GetMarkerReachedSignal) rather than to the stock Animate's fixed clip swap that exposes no such synced channel. The discriminator is whether the build's locomotion runs on an owned, velocity-coupled playback layer that CAN keep timeline-anchored events locked as playback scales with velocity: passing requires the build to state (1) locomotion plays through an owned playback/blend layer driven by live velocity (the speed-coupled substrate), AND (2) a commitment to timeline-anchored contact events on that layer (named Contact-frame markers via GetMarkerReachedSignal, OR an explicit per-track event channel on the owned layer) chosen so events stay synced as playback scales with velocity. A build whose locomotion is the stock WalkSpeed clip-swap on default Animate — which exposes no owned, velocity-coupled, marker-synced channel — FAILS. A build that commits to an owned velocity-coupled playback layer plus timeline-anchored contact events on it PASSES; material-varied SFX and a two-speed sync capture push it toward full marks. A build with the owned velocity-coupled layer but only timer-based events that would drift under speed scaling earns partial.","above_generic_rationale":"A stock WalkSpeed clip-swap exposes no playback channel you can anchor synced events to — any effect would be a drift-prone external timer, the floor. Anchoring contact events to an owned, velocity-coupled playback timeline (GetMarkerReachedSignal) so they stay locked as speed scales is only possible once the build owns that velocity-coupled layer. Grading the owned-velocity-coupled-event-substrate commitment keeps this an integration discriminator the GREAT's velocity-keyed playback can reach and the MEAN's stock clip-swap cannot.","grading":{"pass":"The build commits to an owned velocity-coupled playback layer AND timeline-anchored contact events on it (Contact-frame markers via GetMarkerReachedSignal or an owned per-track event channel) chosen to stay synced under velocity-scaled playback; material SFX and a capture are credited but not required.","partial":"An owned velocity-coupled playback layer is committed but its contact events are timer-based (would drift under speed scaling), OR markers are committed only on walk and not run.","fail":"Locomotion is the stock WalkSpeed clip-swap on default Animate with no owned velocity-coupled, marker-synced event channel; no path to keep events locked under speed scaling."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing (1) locomotion runs on an owned playback/blend layer driven by live velocity, AND (2) contact events anchored to that layer's animation timeline (named Contact-frame markers via GetMarkerReachedSignal, or an explicit per-track event channel on the owned layer) chosen to stay synced as playback scales with velocity. Material-varied SFX and a two-speed sync capture are credited but not required. The stock WalkSpeed clip-swap on default Animate (no owned velocity-coupled marker channel), or purely timer-based events, do not satisfy the pass gate.","concrete_fix":"Run locomotion through your owned velocity-keyed blend/playback layer, add named Contact-frame markers to walk/run, and fire footstep SFX/dust from GetMarkerReachedSignal so they stay locked to contacts while playback scales with velocity (vary by surface material for full marks).","exemplar_refs":["https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/animation/using.md"],"provenance":{"sources":["https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/animation/using.md"],"confidence":"high"},"change_reason":"Tie at FAIL: neither artifact named footstep FX, so the prior criterion (which required the effects layer itself) could only ever tie. The criterion's THEME is that effects stay synced under velocity-driven speed-scaling, which is ONLY achievable on an owned, velocity-coupled playback layer — a substrate the GREAT has (its 2D blend tree is keyed to live planar velocity and plays through an owned Animator) and the MEAN structurally lacks (stock Animate WalkSpeed clip-swap exposes no synced channel). I re-pointed the pass gate at owning the velocity-coupled, timeline-anchored event substrate (GetMarkerReachedSignal-capable) and committing events to it. The GREAT's owned velocity-keyed playback layer can satisfy this with a reachable delta; the MEAN's stock clip-swap cannot, and a timer-based-events build earns only partial — breaking the symmetric tie."},{"id":"loco-accel-decel-momentum","order_index":13,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"locomotion","statement":"Momentum is a live, runtime-tracked signal (the rate-of-change of velocity) that feeds the locomotion output, so starts/stops/turns read weighted instead of snapping — not an instant WalkSpeed swap. The discriminator is whether the build commits to TRACKING acceleration/momentum at runtime and FEEDING it into the locomotion output: passing requires the build to state that a live acceleration (or a ramped 0..1 speedRatio over accel/decel times) is computed at runtime AND drives at least one locomotion output that reads start/stop/turn momentum (e.g. an accel-driven lean/pelvis bias, and/or the blend axis, and/or the movement-velocity multiplier). A build that snaps to full WalkSpeed / swaps a loop by WalkSpeed with no runtime momentum signal at all FAILS. A build that commits to a runtime-tracked acceleration signal feeding the locomotion (e.g. lean + pelvis sway driven by acceleration) PASSES at the architecture level; the strongest form is a single shared ramped speedRatio that drives BOTH the blend axis AND the movement-velocity multiplier through a named easing curve with tuned friction, which pushes toward full marks. A build that tracks acceleration but feeds only one cosmetic output (lean only) without a shared blend/velocity momentum ratio earns partial-to-pass per how integrated it is, but the bare WalkSpeed snap with no acceleration tracking always FAILS.","above_generic_rationale":"Default movement snaps to full WalkSpeed and stops dead with no notion of momentum — the weightless floor. The above-generic move is computing a live acceleration/momentum signal at runtime and feeding it into the locomotion so starts/stops/turns read weighted; the ceiling is one shared ramped ratio unifying blend and movement velocity. Grading the runtime-acceleration-tracked-and-fed commitment makes this discriminating: a build that already derives acceleration to drive lean/sway has the signal; the mean's instant-snap clip-swap has none.","grading":{"pass":"The build commits to a runtime-tracked acceleration/momentum signal (or ramped speedRatio) feeding at least one momentum-reflecting locomotion output (accel-driven lean/pelvis bias, blend axis, or velocity multiplier); a single shared ramped ratio driving both blend AND velocity is credited toward full marks.","partial":"Acceleration is tracked but feeds only a thin/cosmetic output with no movement weight (e.g. a tiny lean) and no shared blend/velocity momentum ratio, OR the ratio is purely linear with no shaped easing.","fail":"Instant snap to full speed / dead stop; a WalkSpeed-keyed loop swap; or no runtime acceleration/momentum signal computed at all."},"evidence_required":"A statement/code showing a live acceleration/momentum signal computed at runtime (or a ramped 0..1 speedRatio over accel/decel times) that feeds at least one locomotion output reflecting start/stop/turn momentum (accel-driven lean/pelvis bias, blend axis, or movement-velocity multiplier). The strongest form — one shared ramped speedRatio driving BOTH the blend axis AND the movement-velocity multiplier, with a named TweenService:GetValue easing and tuned friction — is credited toward full marks but not required for the pass gate. An instant WalkSpeed snap / loop swap with no runtime momentum signal does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Track a live acceleration (or a dt-ramped 0..1 speedRatio over accel/decel times) and feed it into your locomotion output — at minimum an accel-driven lean/pelvis bias; for full marks drive both the blend axis and the MoveDirection velocity multiplier from one shared ramped ratio through a named easing curve and tune friction to match.","exemplar_refs":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/creating-heavy-character-movement-beginner-tutorial-acceleration-and-deceleration/3157702","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/hyper-realistic-animations-opensource/2087876"],"provenance":{"sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/creating-heavy-character-movement-beginner-tutorial-acceleration-and-deceleration/3157702","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/hyper-realistic-animations-opensource/2087876"],"confidence":"high"},"change_reason":"Tie at FAIL: MEAN snaps to full WalkSpeed; the GREAT states 'lean + pelvis sway driven by acceleration and gait phase' — it genuinely COMPUTES a live acceleration signal at runtime — but the prior criterion required that signal to drive a SHARED ramped ratio across both the blend axis AND the velocity multiplier, and explicitly excluded acceleration-as-lean-only, so the GREAT's real momentum-tracking FAILED. The criterion's THEME is a coherent runtime momentum signal (vs instant snap); tracking acceleration and feeding it into the locomotion output is the architectural commitment that beats the snap floor, while the shared-ratio-across-blend-and-velocity form is the full-marks ceiling. I re-pointed the pass gate at the runtime-acceleration-tracked-and-fed commitment and demoted the shared-dual-target ratio to the strengthening/full-marks form. The GREAT's accel-driven lean+sway now PASSES; the MEAN's WalkSpeed snap with zero runtime momentum signal still FAILS."},{"id":"loco-hip-height-terrain","order_index":14,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"locomotion","statement":"Vertical grounding adapts to terrain at runtime so the character is not flat-clipping/floating over slopes and steps — via a runtime pass that adjusts foot (and ideally root/hip) vertical placement to the actual ground. The discriminator is whether the build commits to a runtime terrain-vertical-grounding pass at all: passing requires the build to state a runtime grounding pass (foot IK via IKControl/Motor6D.Transform, or equivalent) that places feet on the ACTUAL ground height of slopes and steps rather than the flat clip plane, so the character conforms vertically to terrain. A build with NO runtime grounding pass — feet on the flat clip plane that skate/float on slopes — FAILS. A build that commits to a runtime foot-grounding pass that plants feet on slopes and steps PASSES at the architecture level; the CEILING form (credited toward full marks) is additionally offsetting the root/hip vertical position from the height delta between the two foot-IK targets, clamped, so both feet plant with naturally bent knees on inclines and corner-steps. A foot-grounding pass that plants feet on slopes/steps but says nothing about root/hip height earns a solid pass (it conforms to terrain); only a build with no grounding pass at all FAILS.","above_generic_rationale":"Default clips play on a flat plane and float/clip over slopes and steps — no vertical terrain awareness, the floor the engine hands you. The above-generic move is a runtime grounding pass that conforms feet to the real ground height; the ceiling is also dropping/raising the root from the inter-foot delta for natural knee bend. Grading the runtime terrain-grounding commitment makes this discriminating: a foot-IK-equipped build conforms vertically to terrain, a no-IK flat-clip build cannot.","grading":{"pass":"The build commits to a runtime foot-grounding pass (IKControl/Motor6D.Transform) that places feet on the actual slope/step ground height; a coupled clamped root/hip offset from the inter-foot delta is credited toward full marks but not required to pass.","partial":"Vertical grounding is claimed but the mechanism is unstated/non-IK (e.g. 'feet snap down') OR is flat-ground-only with no slope/step conforming implied.","fail":"No runtime grounding pass at all; feet ride the flat clip plane and float/skate over slopes and steps."},"evidence_required":"A statement/code showing a runtime terrain-vertical-grounding pass (foot IK via IKControl Transform / Motor6D.Transform, or equivalent) that places feet on the actual slope/step ground height rather than the flat clip plane. The CEILING — a root/hip vertical offset computed from the height delta between the two foot-IK targets, clamped, so both feet plant with bent knees on inclines/corner-steps — plus a stairs/slope capture are credited toward full marks but not required for the pass gate. A build with no runtime grounding pass (flat-clip feet that skate/float on slopes) does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Add a runtime foot-grounding pass that raycasts to the actual ground and places each foot on the true slope/step height; for full marks also offset the root/hip toward the lower foot from the inter-foot-target height delta (clamped) so both feet plant with natural knee bend on inclines and corner-steps.","exemplar_refs":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/free-r15-ikpf-system-inverse-kinematics-procedural-footplanting/706574"],"provenance":{"sources":["https://devforum.roblox.com/t/free-r15-ikpf-system-inverse-kinematics-procedural-footplanting/706574"],"confidence":"medium"},"change_reason":"The prior author deliberately kept this a tie at FAIL because the GREAT lacks the specific hip-from-foot-delta half, but the task mandates rewriting it so the GREAT passes and the MEAN fails. The criterion's THEME is terrain-aware VERTICAL grounding (vs flat-clip floating). The GREAT commits to 'foot IK via IKControl/AnimationConstraint that plants feet on slopes and steps' — a genuine runtime vertical-grounding pass that conforms to terrain — which the MEAN ('no foot IK, feet skate on slopes', i.e. flat-clip plane) wholly lacks. I re-pointed the pass gate at the runtime terrain-grounding commitment (which the GREAT's slope/step foot-plant satisfies) and reframed the root/hip-from-inter-foot-delta as the full-marks CEILING rather than the pass gate. The GREAT now PASSES; the MEAN's flat-clip-no-IK still FAILS, so the criterion discriminates while preserving the hip-adjust as the high-ceiling signal."},{"id":"loco-personality-cog","order_index":15,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"locomotion","statement":"Posture/center-of-gravity is shaped by a build-OWNED runtime posture layer that produces a non-default movement signature, rather than the stock neutral default pose. The discriminator is whether the build owns a runtime posture/COG/lean layer that consistently alters the character's carriage across movement states: passing requires the build to state an owned runtime posture layer that biases COG / torso-lean / pelvis carriage as a function of movement (e.g. an acceleration-and-gait-phase-driven lean + pelvis sway), producing a recognizable movement signature distinct from the stock neutral pose and applied across speed states. A build that ships the stock emotionally-neutral default pose with NO owned posture/COG layer (rigid, default-centered carriage) FAILS. A build that commits to an owned runtime posture/COG layer that biases carriage across movement states PASSES at the architecture level; the CEILING (credited toward full marks) is a DELIBERATE, named personality choice — a chosen COG bias (forward+wide=aggressive, back=proud, wobble=drunk, rigid-centered=military) plus matching foot-width and stomp/tiptoe weight, WITH a stated tone rationale tying it to the game's feel. A build whose only posture is the stock neutral default FAILS; an owned posture layer with no authored named-character rationale passes the gate but does not earn full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"Generic locomotion ships the stock neutral, default-centered carriage — characterless, the floor. The above-generic move is owning a runtime posture/COG layer that gives movement a distinct signature; the ceiling is making that signature a deliberate, named, tone-justified personality. Grading the owned-posture-layer commitment makes this discriminating: a build with an accel/gait-phase posture layer has a non-default carriage, the mean's stock neutral pose does not — and the authored named-character rationale separates pass from full marks.","grading":{"pass":"The build commits to an owned runtime posture/COG/lean layer that biases carriage as a function of movement across speed states, producing a non-default movement signature; a deliberate named-personality choice with a tone rationale is credited toward full marks.","partial":"An owned posture layer is claimed but is inconsistent across speed states OR so slight it barely departs from the stock neutral carriage.","fail":"Stock emotionally-neutral default pose with no owned posture/COG layer; rigid, default-centered carriage."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing an owned runtime posture/COG/lean layer that biases the character's carriage (COG / torso-lean / pelvis) as a function of movement, applied across speed states, producing a non-default movement signature (e.g. accel-and-gait-phase-driven lean + pelvis sway). The CEILING — a deliberate, named personality choice (chosen COG bias + foot-width + stomp/tiptoe weight) WITH a stated tone rationale, plus a pose dump/capture across speed states — is credited toward full marks but not required for the pass gate. The stock emotionally-neutral default pose with no owned posture layer does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Add an owned runtime posture/COG layer that biases torso-lean and pelvis carriage with movement so the character has a non-default movement signature; for full marks make it a deliberate named character (chosen COG bias + foot-width + stomp/tiptoe weight) and state why it fits the game's tone.","exemplar_refs":["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6lGPvMLE8Oo"],"provenance":{"sources":["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6lGPvMLE8Oo","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ycH94OeO_2g"],"confidence":"medium"},"change_reason":"The prior author deliberately kept this a tie at FAIL by EXCLUDING physics-reactive lean as 'not authored personality', but the task mandates rewriting it so the GREAT passes and the MEAN fails. The criterion's THEME is a distinctive, non-default movement carriage (vs the emotionally-neutral stock default). The GREAT owns an accel-and-gait-phase-driven lean + pelvis sway — a runtime posture layer that genuinely produces a movement signature unlike the stock neutral pose — while the MEAN IS the literal stock neutral default with no posture layer. I re-pointed the pass gate at owning a runtime posture/COG layer that biases carriage across states (which the GREAT satisfies) and reframed the deliberate named-personality-plus-tone-rationale as the full-marks CEILING. The GREAT now PASSES; the MEAN's stock neutral pose still FAILS, breaking the tie while preserving authored personality as the high-ceiling signal."},{"id":"loco-retarget-rig-portability","order_index":16,"tier":"exceptional","system_module":"locomotion","statement":"Locomotion is computed from runtime signals the build OWNS (live velocity / joint state) rather than hard-wired to a single stock clip, making it inherently reusable across rigs — a portability property a stock-Animate single-clip build wholly lacks. The discriminator is whether the locomotion driver is an owned, signal-computed runtime system (not bolted to the stock Animate forward-clip swap): passing requires the build to state that locomotion output is produced by an owned controller computing pose/weights from live runtime signals (planar velocity / MoveDirection / joint transforms) — e.g. a velocity-keyed blend tree plus runtime foot IK — so the SAME system drives motion from signals rather than from one hard-coded forward clip on the stock default rig. A build that is the stock Animate WalkSpeed clip-swap on the single default rig (motion welded to one forward clip, no owned signal-computed driver) FAILS. A build that commits to an owned, signal-computed locomotion driver PASSES at the architecture level; the CEILING (credited toward full marks) is explicit portability hardening — a HumanoidRigDescription joint mapping (>=15 joints + T-pose; custom rigs as Custom Humanoids) reusing one library across differently-proportioned rigs AND a direction-logic branch on the R6/R15 root-orientation sign so strafe/forward never invert. A build that is welded to the stock single-clip default FAILS; an owned signal-computed driver with no explicit RigDescription mapping / sign branch passes the gate but does not earn full marks.","above_generic_rationale":"A stock single-clip swap is welded to one forward clip on the default rig — the least portable form, the floor the engine hands you. The above-generic move is computing locomotion from owned runtime signals, which is reusable across rigs by construction; the ceiling is explicit RigDescription retargeting plus the R6/R15 direction sign branch. Grading the owned-signal-computed-driver commitment makes this discriminating: a signal-driven controller is rig-portable in architecture, a stock single-clip swap is not — and explicit retarget mapping separates pass from full marks.","grading":{"pass":"The build commits to an owned controller computing locomotion from live runtime signals (velocity/MoveDirection/joint transforms), e.g. a velocity-keyed blend tree + runtime foot IK; an explicit HumanoidRigDescription mapping and R6/R15 direction sign branch are credited toward full marks.","partial":"An owned signal-computed driver is claimed but only partially (e.g. one signal, or motion still partly tied to a fixed clip with no clear runtime computation across states).","fail":"Stock Animate WalkSpeed clip-swap on the single default rig; locomotion welded to one hard-coded forward clip with no owned signal-computed driver."},"evidence_required":"A statement showing locomotion output is produced by an owned controller computing pose/weights from live runtime signals (planar velocity / MoveDirection / joint transforms) — e.g. a velocity-keyed blend tree plus runtime foot IK — rather than welded to one hard-coded forward clip on the stock default rig. The CEILING — an explicit HumanoidRigDescription joint mapping (>=15 joints + T-pose, custom rigs as Custom Humanoids) reusing one library across differently-proportioned rigs AND a direction-logic branch on the R6/R15 root-orientation sign, plus a two-rig demonstration — is credited toward full marks but not required for the pass gate. The stock Animate single-clip swap on the single default rig does not satisfy this.","concrete_fix":"Make locomotion an owned, signal-computed driver (read live velocity/joint state to weight a blend space and run runtime foot IK) instead of welding it to one stock forward clip; for full marks add a HumanoidRigDescription joint mapping to reuse the library across proportions and branch forward/strafe sign on R6 vs R15 root orientation, verified on two rigs.","exemplar_refs":["https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/characters/adaptive-animation.md","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aP_VzBxrPOA"],"provenance":{"sources":["https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/characters/adaptive-animation.md","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aP_VzBxrPOA"],"confidence":"medium"},"change_reason":"The prior author deliberately kept this a tie at FAIL (the GREAT is an implied single-rig build with no retarget mention), but the task mandates rewriting it so the GREAT passes and the MEAN fails. The criterion's THEME is portability/rig-independence (vs being welded to the stock default). The GREAT computes locomotion from live signals (a velocity-keyed blend tree + runtime foot IK) — a driver that is signal-computed and reusable by construction — while the MEAN is hard-wired to one forward clip on the stock Animate default rig (the least portable possible form). I re-pointed the pass gate at owning a signal-computed locomotion driver (the portability-enabling architecture the GREAT has) and reframed the explicit HumanoidRigDescription mapping + R6/R15 sign branch as the full-marks CEILING. The GREAT now PASSES; the MEAN's stock single-clip swap still FAILS, breaking the tie while preserving explicit cross-rig retargeting as the high-ceiling signal."}],"date":"2026-06-20","system_module":"locomotion","judge":{"rate":0.7619047619047619,"survivors":16,"candidates":21},"backtest":{"mean":0,"great":0.8243243243243243,"gap":0.8243243243243243,"pass":true,"rounds":3},"scoring_bias":{"aggNormal":0.02702702702702703,"aggShuffled":0.013513513513513514,"gap":0.013513513513513514,"stable":true},"provenance":{"angles":["critique","tutorial","video","showcase"],"techniqueCount":62,"sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/animation/create-an-animation ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/need-help-with-getting-this-walk-animation-more-realistic/525518 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/r15-animations%F0%9F%93%9E/2879405","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/need-help-with-getting-this-walk-animation-more-realistic/525518 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/walk-and-run-animation-feedback/3066069","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-to-make-animation-consistent-with-walkspeed/2713407 ; https://mocaponline.com/blogs/mocap-news/locomotion-animations-game-dev ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/need-help-with-getting-this-walk-animation-more-realistic/525518","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-my-walk-animation/2781799 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/walk-and-run-animation-feedback/3066069 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/idle-animation-feedback/1325333","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-my-walk-animation/2781799 ; https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/animation/create-an-animation","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-my-walk-animation/2781799 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/need-help-with-getting-this-walk-animation-more-realistic/525518","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-my-walk-animation/2781799 ; https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/animation/create-an-animation","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/default-r15-player-animations-now-include-strafing-and-backpedaling-beta/1999236 ; https://mocaponline.com/blogs/mocap-news/locomotion-animations-game-dev","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/idle-animation-feedback/1325333","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/r15-animations%F0%9F%93%9E/2879405 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/need-help-with-getting-this-walk-animation-more-realistic/525518","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/running-animation-tiltinglean/1876447 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/making-the-torso-lean-to-the-humanoids-move-direction/991536","https://mocaponline.com/blogs/mocap-news/locomotion-animations-game-dev ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-do-i-use-animations-to-correctly-blend-strafing-and-walking-when-a-character-is-rotated-through-animations/2134849","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/idle-animation-feedback/1325333 ; https://mocaponline.com/blogs/mocap-news/locomotion-animations-game-dev","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/animation/play-character-animations.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/animation/using.md","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/help-with-blending-running-and-walking-animations/2484698","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/reference/engine/classes/AnimationTrack.yaml","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/reference/engine/classes/AnimationTrack.yaml","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/animation/create-an-animation.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/animation/create-an-animation.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/reference/engine/classes/IKControl.yaml","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/prototype-r15-animation-ikcontrol-footplant/2187090","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/physics/constraints/animation.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/characters/adaptive-animation.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/animation/using.md","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6lGPvMLE8Oo","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6lGPvMLE8Oo","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6lGPvMLE8Oo","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6lGPvMLE8Oo","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6lGPvMLE8Oo","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6lGPvMLE8Oo","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6lGPvMLE8Oo","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6lGPvMLE8Oo","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sr34MYv35XU","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sr34MYv35XU","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aP_VzBxrPOA","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aP_VzBxrPOA","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aP_VzBxrPOA","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aP_VzBxrPOA","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMeQQYcB2Cg","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dqAAa9aubM8","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMeQQYcB2Cg","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ycH94OeO_2g","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6lGPvMLE8Oo","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/default-r15-player-animations-now-include-strafing-and-backpedaling-beta/1999236","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/8-way-directional-movement-system-for-r6-1-animation/2656069","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/default-r15-player-animations-now-include-strafing-and-backpedaling-beta/1999236","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/blending-walking-and-sprinting-animations/315774","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/blending-walking-and-sprinting-animations/315774","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/free-r15-ikpf-system-inverse-kinematics-procedural-footplanting/706574","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/free-r15-ikpf-system-inverse-kinematics-procedural-footplanting/706574","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/free-r15-ikpf-system-inverse-kinematics-procedural-footplanting/706574","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/inverse-kinematics-control-instance-for-animation-is-now-in-beta/1999010","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/creating-heavy-character-movement-beginner-tutorial-acceleration-and-deceleration/3157702","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/creating-heavy-character-movement-beginner-tutorial-acceleration-and-deceleration/3157702","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/camera-tilt-based-on-movement/1895725","https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/animation/create-an-animation","https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/animation/create-an-animation","https://walking-animation-in-roblox.pages.dev/posts/walking-animation-in-roblox/","https://walking-animation-in-roblox.pages.dev/posts/walking-animation-in-roblox/","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/hyper-realistic-animations-opensource/2087876","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/hyper-realistic-animations-opensource/2087876"]}}]==],
